    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P3" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark text:name="_heading=h.dl44m92ptozs"/><text:span text:style-name="T3">Evil Persistence</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P4" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark text:name="bookmark=id.i3gnvyuecuvc"/><text:span text:style-name="T4">A David Jackson Mystery</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P5" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P6" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P6" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P6" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P6" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P5" loext:marker-style-name="T5"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P2" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P7" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T1">Copyright © 2026 Roger Southwick</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P7" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T1">Slate Creek Publishing, LLC</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P7" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T1">All rights reserved.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P8" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T1">ISBN:</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P8" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T1">ISBN-13: </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">VERSION: </text:span><office:annotation loext:resolved="false">
       <dc:creator>Roger Southwick</dc:creator>
       <dc:date>2025-10-04T14:50:52</dc:date>
       <meta:creator-initials>rss</meta:creator-initials>
       <text:p text:style-name="P9"><text:span text:style-name="T6">VERSION</text:span></text:p>
      </office:annotation><office:annotation loext:resolved="false">
       <dc:date>0000-00-00T00:00:00</dc:date>
       <text:p/>
      </office:annotation><text:span text:style-name="T5"><text:modification-date style:data-style-name="N10129">Jul 31, 2026</text:modification-date></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5"><text:s/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5"><text:modification-date style:data-style-name="N10061">16:47:52</text:modification-date></text:span></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10" loext:marker-style-name="T1"/>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P11" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T3">This novel is entirely a work of fiction. The names, characters, and incidents portrayed in it are the work of the author’s imagination. Any resemblance to actual persons, living or dead, or events is entirely coincidental. Any public agencies, institutions, or locations mentioned in the story only serve as a backdrop for the imaginary characters and their fictional actions.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3"/></text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:h text:style-name="P12" text:outline-level="1" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark text:name="Chapter_1_-_Simple_Man"/><text:span text:style-name="T7">Simple Man</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"><text:line-break/></text:span></text:h>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared at the final report from the department inquiry into the shooting of Evan Dixon. He’d read it every day since it came out last week. Again and again. Were they blaming Nicky and him for the incident? Nothing was clear despite the hours of testimony and the endless review.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T9"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax knew what had happened, even though Chief Roberts had voiced his unhappiness with the outcome. He and Nicky tried to connect with Evan. They tried to get him to drop the gun and surrender. But Evan, rather than spending life in prison, chose the coward’s way out, chosing suicide by cop. The rookie patrol officer was so distraught after shooting Evan, that he had decided to leave the department.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T9"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky postponed part of his honeymoon until after they solved the case and left last Saturday with Renee for Europe, where they explored their respective family roots. While Nicky was enjoying the scenery, Captain Holcomb had partnered Jax with a brash, young detective named John Blumfield.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield had had five partners in as many months, always so cocky, so sure he was right, not listening to the more experienced partner. Jax had struggled to maintain his cool with the youngster. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">During the last robbery they were on, Blumfield told the captain that Jax knew nothing about investigations. However, it was Jax who had caught the perpetrator when he noticed the man wearing a distinctive ring in the surveillance video. It didn’t help matters when Holcomb thanked Jax for the great work and ignored Blumfield. Since that day, Blumfield had been more than cold, teetering on the brink of aggression.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The desk phone rang, dragging Jax back to the present.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Jackson,” he answered and started jotting down notes.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A pause. Then: “Okay, we’re on our way.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He hung up. “Come on, John. We’ve got a robbery to investigate.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Again?” Blumfield groaned. “We already have five open cases. Why us?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shot him a look. The words rose fast, bitter, and sharp in his mind:</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T10">That’s because I’m working while you’re talking.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He didn’t say it. Not because he couldn’t, but because it wouldn’t help. And he was too tired to fight every battle this kid wanted to start.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They made the short drive in silence, arriving at the Hi-Way Market convenience store on Aurora Ave, not four blocks from where they had taken down Dixon. Several patrol officers and CSU were on the scene, waiting for them.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Inside, the store was a mess. A toppled chip rack near the counter. Food scattered in one aisle. Broken bottles of some sticky goop spread out on the floor near the motor oil. The cash register hanging open, empty of the cash. Reflections on the floor from the cooler door glinted under the harsh fluorescents. The CSU technicians were busy collecting evidence and dusting for fingerprints.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They returned outside to where the clerk, a woman in her mid-forties, stood, arms folded across her chest. Eyes puffy. Bruises were forming on her arm. Still shaken.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield flashed his badge at the clerk as if he were showing off a trophy. “Detective John Blumfield, SPD. This is Lieutenant Jackson. Are you the one who called it in?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The woman nodded, arms still crossed tight across her chest. She looked as if she might fold in on herself.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Name?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Darla Krantz.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Alright, Darla, I need you to tell me what happened. No guessing. No ‘I think.’ Just facts.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Her lips parted, then closed. “It was … fast. He came in yelling and pointed a gun at me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield talked over her, “Small gun? Big one? What caliber? Was it loaded?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She flinched. “I … I don’t know. It was black. Maybe a revolver?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You may have seen a revolver pointed at you, and you can’t be sure?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He told me to empty the drawer,” she said, voice cracking.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield didn’t slow down. “And you did?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Darla stuttered out her response, “I … I had to. He said he’d shoot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah, that’s how robberies work,” he muttered, scribbling notes.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Her face flushed with anger beneath the fear. “You think I’m making this up?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lady, right now, I’ve got more useful info from the chip rack he knocked over.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax cut in, his voice harsh and firm. “That’s enough, Blumfield! Wait for me outside.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield raised an eyebrow, didn’t argue, and walked out of the store.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to the clerk, his tone gentle. “I apologize for how he treated you, ma’am. I’m sorry, but need to ask if you can describe the person? Race, build, anything that stood out?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She took a shaky breath, grateful. “Caucasian, I think. Kind of short and thinnish. Limped a little. Gray hoodie with a torn sleeve.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “You’re doing great, Ms. Krantz. Did you see any jewelry? Scars? Tattoos?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He had a small scar … here,” she pointed from the corner of her left eye down to her jaw. “I didn’t notice it at first, but when he leaned in, I caught sight of it in the light.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded, writing. “Any smell? Cologne? Cigarettes?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. A musty smell. And sweat. Strong.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Which direction did he run?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She paused. “Out the door and turned north on this side of the street.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax jotted it down without looking up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thank you,” Jax said. “Again, I apologize for my partner. We’ll be in touch.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The two patrol officers lingered near the entrance, one watching the clerk, the other scanning the crowd, which was maintaining a respectful distance.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to Blumfield. “You ever treat a witness that way again, and I’ll get you busted down to doing patrol at the Porta-Johns at the rodeo.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield bristled. “I don’t have time for listening to someone like that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pointed his finger at the young detective. “You need to get this through your thick skull, John. You need to treat the victims with much more kindness and respect; otherwise, you won’t get anything out of them. They are scared, and not trained observers. Got it?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield clenched his fists, as if he thought about hitting Jax for a moment, but then his hands relaxed and the young detective nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t press. He was tired of pressing. He just turned, walking toward the sidewalk, scanning storefronts and checking for cameras mounted under awnings and above doors. His phone buzzed in his pocket. A group text from dispatch:</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T10">UPDATE: Patrol reports possible sighting of suspect near 91st and Aurora.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He typed a quick reply: </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">Jackson will follow on foot. Will advise.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As he turned back towards Bloomfield, Jax saw the patrol sergeant heading his way.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“John, start canvassing this side of the block first. Ask shop owners if they saw someone running by in a gray hoodie. Ask for a copy of their surveillance video. I’ll check with the sergeant and catch up with you in a few minutes.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“On it,” Blumfield muttered and turned up the street.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax watched him go. Then he looked down at his notebook: one description, one direction, one possibility. It wasn’t much. But it was something.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As he looked up, the officer approached. Jax recognized him from roll call.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sergeant Ramirez?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Hey, Jax. Here’s the surveillance footage,” he said. “Store manager pulled the last four hours for you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The officer didn’t walk off. “Anything else, Sergeant?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Clerk’s still pretty shaken. Your partner came in hot.” He hesitated. “She wants to file a complaint, and she asked if you could be the one to follow up.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Please take the information for the complaint, and let her know I won’t subject her to that arrogance again. Here’s my card for her. When you get the complaint ready to file, I’ll have an addendum to add to it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Jax,” Ramirez nodded and walked away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As he pocketed the flash drive, his mind wandered back to what the clerk had said: that the guy ran north, on this side of the street.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">But as he stood there, watching Blumfield disappear into a store on this side, a familiar sound intruded in his thoughts. in his ear.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Check the other side.” The Voice whispered, soft, certain and familiar.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As usual, there was no one around him. Traffic hummed, brakes squealed two blocks down, and a garbage truck hissed past.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Walking across the street, Jax proceeded on the sidewalk, his experienced eye scanning shops for cameras, especially those across from the Hi-Way Market. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The first store, a nail salon, had a sign in the window: Cameras in use. Jax stepped inside and flashed his badge to the woman at the counter.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m looking into a robbery that happened across the street about an hour ago,” he said. “I’m hoping your exterior camera caught something.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The woman didn’t hesitate. “You’re lucky,” she said, tapping a remote. “It only backs up four hours. I’ll pull it for you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">While she worked, Jax glanced toward the storefront window. From here, he had a clear line of sight to the market. If the guy had bolted and tried to cross, darting between cars, this camera might’ve caught it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She handed him a thumb drive. “Hope it’s helpful.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Appreciate it,” Jax said, handing her his card. “If you remember seeing anything, like a man running, wearing a hoodie, please call me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped back out and kept walking, passed a closed pawn shop and a boarded-up travel agency. His eyes checked every awning, black plastic dome, and metal bracket that might hold a lens.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He was a few stores down the street at a dry-cleaning business. Yellowing blinds in the windows. Buzzing neon sign. A “Help Wanted” flier curling at the edges.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped inside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The place was empty except for a man folding clothes behind the counter. Jax showed his badge again and recited the same line.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The man nodded. “Camera’s broken, but… hold on. My cousin was out front smoking when someone ran by like a bat outta hell. Said the guy ran with a limp and almost knocked him over.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s pulse ticked up. “Was this maybe an hour ago?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sounds right.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Can I talk to your cousin?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not till tonight. He had to head off to his other job. But I’ll text him. You got a number?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave him a card. “Have him call me anytime.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Will do,” the guy said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thank you very much,” Jax nodded and stepped back outside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Across the street, the yellow glow of the Hi-Way Market spilled onto the sidewalk. Officers still moved inside like shadows. Blumfield was nowhere in sight. Jax thought he was halfway through a conversation he wasn’t listening to. Jax stood a moment longer, adjusting his coat, tucked the flash drive deeper into his pocket, and kept moving north.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Half a block up, past a closed vape shop, he spotted a small bodega with an outdoor ice machine and a flickering sign: OPEN 24 HOURS. Above the door, Jax saw a camera mounted just beneath the gutter, angled out toward the sidewalk.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped inside; the cooler holding the drinks hummed away in the back of the store.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A teenager stood behind the counter, tapping on a phone.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Is the owner around?” Jax asked, showing his badge to the teen.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yep, my uncle’s in the back. Want me to get him?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Please.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The teen disappeared through a curtain. A few seconds later, a stocky man in his fifties emerged, wiping his with a rag. Jax showed his badge and laid out the story again ... robbery across the street, suspect running northbound, gray hoodie, scar on the face.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The man frowned. “I think he went right past here. I was outside smoking when it happened.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Did you see his face?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Just a glance. Limped, like you said. He ducked into that alley ...” He pointed. “Between here and the Thai place. Didn’t come out, far as I know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s eyes narrowed. “You have camera footage?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I do. Inside and out. You want it?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax smiled and nodded. “More than you know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As Jax reached the mouth of the alley, Blumfield came striding across the street as if he’d cracked the case. “Clerk at the last place thinks his outside cam may have caught a glimpse of our guy. CSU is pulling it now as the clerk didn’t know how to access the video. You’re welcome.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax filed the smugness away. It wasn’t the first time. It wouldn’t be the last. Instead, he studied the dark alley, waiting for his eyes to adjust to the shadows. It stretched out ahead, narrow, hemmed in by mismatched walls ... brick on one wall, grimy concrete on the other side, and the back wall. Dumpsters lined the way like sleeping beasts. A broken pallet leaned against the wall, half-rotted. The air smelled of stale grease and old beer, as if the alley had been on a ten-year bender.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Clerk at the bodega says the guy ducked in here,” Jax said. “Didn’t come out again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield raised an eyebrow, unimpressed. “So what, he did a Houdini?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Jax said, stepping into the alley. “This alley is a dead end. Maybe he’s still in there.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield followed, muttering, “Hope you brought your flashlight. Mine’s in the car.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax ignored him, pulling a small light from his jacket pocket and casting the beam along the edges of the alley, angling it low, sweeping. Always low first. People forget to look down, but blood, footprints, cigarette butts ... they don’t hover midair.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">About halfway in, something caught his eye. Scuff marks on the damp concrete just beyond a tipped-over trash can. Close to the wall, as if someone had slid against it or braced themselves.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Here,” Jax whispered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield moved closer. “You think he stopped here?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Maybe,” Jax said, crouching. He touched two fingers to the mark, then held them to the light. “See, it’s recent.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You sure that’s not from a raccoon or something?” Blumfield said, but his voice didn’t carry conviction.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax straightened and pointed at the outline on the ground. “Have you ever seen raccoons wear size eight boots?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield said nothing, but met Jax’s eyes with silent recognition and nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax swept the flashlight upward, illuminating a rusted fire escape clinging to the wall above them like a relic from another era. The bottom rung was missing, but someone had scuffed the second one, wearing its edge smooth.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Maybe he climbed up,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Looks like a death trap.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Desperate men take stupid risks,” Jax replied, eyeing the wall. “Could’ve pulled himself up. Limping or not.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They both turned, gazing upward. The escape zigzagged toward a rooftop two stories above. Nothing stirred in the dusk, but Jax could feel it again ... that tension threading through his ribs, whispering beneath his skin.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Left,” The Voice came as a silent whisper.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He turned, the beam catching a metal door set back from the main path. Not part of the fire escape. The door was low, hidden behind stacked crates and a sagging canopy of vines that shouldn’t have survived city air.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield squinted, trying to see anything in the inky darkness, and asked, “Do you see something?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not yet,” Jax answered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They approached the door. It was old, metal, and painted a dull green with spots of rust bleeding through. The lock appeared to be broken, and the frame was bent inward. The handle was clean and shiny. Someone had opened it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pressed his fingers to the handle, then nodded to Blumfield and whispered so that only the young man could hear him. “Cover me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">For the first time all day, Bloomfield didn’t have a quip or a complaint. Jax watched him step to the side, right hand resting near his weapon. Just as he was trained to do.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax drew his sidearm, exhaled once, and pushed the door open.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The space beyond was dark ... just the edge of gray light seeping through a small window near the roof. Shelving units crowded the corners, some broken, others full of cleaning supplies.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Empty ... at first glance.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then a sound. A faint shuffle.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax froze, listening intently.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Another soft noise penetrated the space.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He moved forward, slow and deliberate, the beam of light slicing through the dark until it landed on a small body huddled behind an upturned mop bucket and a crate of industrial cleaner.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Gray hoodie. Scarred face. Leg curled beneath him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax raised his voice. “Don’t move.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The youngster flinched but didn’t bolt.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield stepped in behind him, weapon trained. “Hands where we can see them.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Dropping the child’s backpack, the young teen lifted his hands, trembling, his lips quivering. “Don’t shoot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax lowered his weapon a fraction but kept the tension in his voice. “What’s your name?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin,” he whispered. “Calvin Spence.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Alright, Calvin,” Jax said. “Let’s talk.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P15" loext:marker-style-name="T11"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="2_The_Kids_Are_Not_Alright"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">The Kids Are Not Alright</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="2_The_Kids_Are_Not_Alright"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin Spence sat with his knees drawn up, hoodie sleeves tugged over his fists like he could disappear into the fabric if he just pulled hard enough. He hadn’t said much ... not since they brought him in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood in the corner of the small interview room, arms crossed, eyes on the floor. The kid hadn’t asked for a lawyer when they read him his rights. He hadn’t screamed or denied anything. He just sat there trembling, backpack at his feet. Ms. Greer, the social worker, sat in the chair next to the youngster.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t sit right away. He watched first ... how Calvin’s shoelace had snapped and been knotted, how his backpack zipper was broken and safety-pinned shut. Clues that spoke louder than any confession.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">On the table: a bruised banana, two apples, a loaf of off-brand bread, and a pack of cheese slices. Not even enough for a full day, if you knew hunger.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">No money. No weapon. No threat.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Where’d you get the food, Calvin?” Jax asked gently, pulling out the chair across from him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s voice was raw, barely there. “Hi-Way Market.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You take any cash?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He shook his head, quick and terrified. “Didn’t even look in the register. Just grabbed the bag and ran. I told the lady I was sorry. I ...” His voice splintered like it hurt to speak. His eyes filled with tears.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“My brother hasn’t eaten since yesterday.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax studied him. The hunched shoulders. The grime on his sleeves. The silence that wasn’t guilt, but shame.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then, quietly, Jax asked, “How old are you?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin hesitated, like the answer might make it worse. “Thirteen.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax blinked. That was two years younger than he’d guessed. Suddenly, the hoodie seemed to swallow the kid whole.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Thirteen.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Just a kid. Jax remembered sleeping in his mom’s car once, the ache in his empty stomach sharper than the cold.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You live nearby?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Two blocks from where you found me.” A pause. “It’s just me and Micah. He’s six.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat back slowly. “Your parents?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin lowered his head. “Gone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ms. Greer sat quietly, her hands folded tightly in her lap. Watching. Listening and not interrupting.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t ask how. Not yet.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A cold knot settled in Jax’s chest. He looked again at the food on the table. Just enough to split between two mouths for a day. The expired school identification card. The gaunt face from a lack of food.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield, standing behind the glass, frowned. He flipped back through his notes, reviewing the clerk’s statement about the gun, the threats, and the cash. None of it matched.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s jaw worked, tension flaring behind his temples. He stood, paced once, then stopped. Turned back. “Calvin, listen to me. Please tell me the truth, all of it. Did you ever have a weapon? Or something that the clerk could have mistaken as a weapon?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No, sir.” He looked up for the first time. “I swear.”.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Okay, get comfortable,” Jax said as he left the interrogation room. The kid’s eyes followed him, silently pleading. He hated walking away from that kind of silence.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax and Blumfield walked into the small conference room down the hall and closed the door.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Unlike other rooms at the precinct, this one smelled like old coffee and floor wax. Fluorescents buzzed above. The trash can in the corner had accumulated a week’s worth of coffee cups and other garbage.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood at the far end of the table, hands braced on the edge, eyes locked on Blumfield across from him. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What do you think?” Jax asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s a thief.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“How old do you think he is?” Jax asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield snorted. “Old enough to know better.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He says thirteen.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then he’s thirteen and a thief.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s eyes narrowed. “He stole food. No cash. Just food. For his little brother.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield leaned back in his chair, arms folded, like he’d heard it a thousand times. “The clerk said he went to the register. She’s got bruises on her arm. That’s robbery, not shoplifting.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The clerk said he did,” Jax replied. “But the kid swears he didn’t touch the till. Says she grabbed him first, tried to stop him, and he yanked free.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s lying.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You don’t know that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I know the clerk’s a grown woman with a bruised wrist and a surveillance camera behind the counter. And I know we’ve got a full plate of real cases to deal with while you babysit Oliver Twist.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pushed off the table and started pacing. “Have you ever been that hungry, John? So hungry you’d risk getting caught, just to feed your kid brother?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield’s jaw tightened. “We’re not social workers, Jax.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No, we’re cops. Which means we deal with facts. I’m not booking a thirteen-year-old on a robbery charge without seeing that footage. And we need to go look for his little brother, who’s probably all alone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield stood, his voice rising just enough to echo. “You think the clerk made it up?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think we don’t know yet. And until we do, I’m not going to be the one who ruins that kid’s life because we couldn’t be bothered to check a video.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re wasting time.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Better I waste time than wreck a hungry kid’s life on someone else’s bad memory.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield stared at him for a beat, then scoffed, grabbing the door handle. “Fine. You want to watch the damn video? Go for it. But when it shows he did it, you can be the one to read him his rights.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t answer.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Blumfield left, the door slamming shut behind him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood alone in the quiet, the buzzing lights above him, and the echo of his instincts pressing harder than ever. He wasn’t sure what burned more ... his anger or the fear that the system he served might crush a kid before the truth ever came out.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The lighting in the Technical Response Unit was dimmer than the rest of the precinct. The walls were sterile white, the room humming with machines, and the low murmur of techs half-buried in monitors. Jax stood behind the desk of a woman named Priya, who didn’t look up as she queued the footage.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Hi-Way Market, three angles,” she said, fingers tapping. “Timestamp starts five minutes before the 911 call.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “Thanks.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The screen flickered, then steadied. Grainy footage rolled.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The front entrance showed Calvin entering ... head down, hands in his sleeves. He paused by the produce bins, lingered too long at the bread rack. One apple into the backpack, then another. Cheese. Bread. Banana. No fumbling. No rush.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin never went near the register.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Not once.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The camera behind the counter showed the clerk stepping out from behind it and intercepting Calvin by the door. The clerk’s hand shot out and grabbed the strap of his backpack. Calvin twisted, startled, and nearly fell before pulling away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The clerk stumbled, her arm hitting the rack behind her.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin ran out the door. Camera two showed him heading across the street and to the north.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then, Jax narrowed his eyes, and the clerk turned. Looked straight at the register. Paused. Checked the camera.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">And opened the till.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">It happened in a blink. She reached in, pulled out bills, and stuffed them into her purse, which was stashed below the counter. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Priya hit pause. Silence buzzed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You see that?” she asked quietly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I saw it,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A slow burn rose in Jax’s gut. Not just anger ... betrayal.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She made it up,” he said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I already uploaded the video to the case folder.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks. And burn one for me, too.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As Priya worked, Jax stood still, jaw clenched, hands on his hips.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The clerk lied. The kid was innocent.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">And now they had proof.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He thought of Calvin, still sitting in that interview room with his sleeves pulled over his fists like he could hide from the world. He thought of Micah ... six years old and probably alone.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">This wasn’t just about a sandwich anymore.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax returned to the interview room. Calvin still sat curled in the same position, smaller now under the weight of waiting. His sleeves were still tugged down, but at least his fists had loosened a little. The food still sat untouched on the table. The social worker, Ms. Greer, hadn’t moved much either, her hands still folded in her lap, calm but alert.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped into the room quietly, the door clicking shut behind him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked up. Not hopeful ... just wary, bracing for more bad news.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax knelt beside him, not across the table this time. Level.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We watched the video,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s mouth opened slightly, eyes wide. “And?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You didn’t take any money. And you didn’t have a weapon.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A shuddered breath left the boy’s chest like his ribs finally loosened. “She said ... she told you I ...”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She lied,” Jax said softly. “And we’ve got it on video. You’re not going to jail, Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin blinked fast, swallowing hard.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ms. Greer put her hand on the youngster’s shoulder and smiled at him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But we need to find your brother. Right now.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded quickly. “He’s home. I left cartoons on for him. I locked the door like I always do.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned in a little. “Good. That’s good. What’s the address, Calvin?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin hesitated for a breath, then rattled it off. “Grant Court Apartments. 412 Westburn. Room C517. That’s building C, floor 5, unit 17.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “All right. Sit tight a little longer. We’re going to get him, okay? Hand me the key.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to Ms. Greer. “You want to go for a ride with me?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The social worker stood immediately, nodding in agreement. “Absolutely.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t even glance toward the squad room where Blumfield sat behind a desk, probably stewing.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Come on,” he said. “Let’s bring Micah some real food.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">It only took Jax and Ms. Greer about fifteen minutes to reach the apartment, locate the building, and climb the rickety stairs to the fifth floor. The hallway smelled like mold and boiled cabbage. Ms. Greer covered her nose discreetly as they passed flickering overhead lights and peeling paint. Jax said nothing, just counting doors as they walked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">C517.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He knocked ... softly at first, then harder. “Micah? It’s okay. I’m a police officer. I’m a friend of your brother’s.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">No answer.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He hesitated a moment and looked at Ms. Greer before he unlocked the door with the key that Calvin had given him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The door creaked open into dim light and stale air. A flickering television cast blue shadows across the room, a cartoon playing soundlessly. A cold draft spilled in from a cracked window, cutting through the room like it belonged somewhere else entirely. Garbage littered the floor: empty food wrappers, a cracked bowl, and a used diaper in the corner. A rat scurried behind the baseboard heater.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax froze, breath shallow. Ms. Greer’s hand found her chest.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then he saw him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A boy on the stained couch, knees pulled up to his chest. Skinny. Still. Wearing only an oversized T-shirt, some stained underwear, and one sock.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Micah?” Jax’s voice softened, dropping low like it might scare the boy into flight.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah blinked at him from behind the hollowed face. Too thin. The kind of thin that screamed that the kid had not just missed a few meals, but missed weeks of meals. His skin was sallow and covered in bites from ants. His eyes were red from the few tears his body could muster.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped in slowly. “Hey, buddy. Your brother Calvin sent us. He’s okay. He’s safe.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah didn’t speak. Just nodded, barely.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ms. Greer knelt beside the couch. “Hi, sweetheart. I’m Ms. Greer. We’re going to help you, okay?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah looked down. His little fingers picked at the hem of his shirt like it was a nervous habit. He didn’t flinch when another rat skittered behind the fridge.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax moved to the kitchen. The fridge was empty ... just a single bottle of ketchup and a box of baking soda. The pantry had nothing but an old jar of salt and a crusted can opener.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He turned away, jaw tight. Took out his phone. “Dispatch, this is Lieutenant Jackson. I need CPS emergency intake and immediate transport to Children’s Medical. One juvenile, approximately six. Severe neglect and malnutrition. Address: Grant Court Apartments, 412 Westburn. C Building, floor 5, unit 17. Advise ETA.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ms. Greer was already wrapping the boy in a blanket from her bag, cooing softly to him. Micah leaned into her touch ... not like a kid who was soothed, but like someone who hadn’t felt safe warmth in too long.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax crouched next to them.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Your brother did good, Micah,” he said with a hitch in his voice. “He tried to take care of you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah’s voice, barely audible: “He always gives me the food first.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah struggled to breathe. After a moment, he sighed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin said if I stayed real quiet, the cartoons would make the day go faster.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax blinked hard, fighting back the tears and the anger. Nodded. “I know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He stood again. The apartment suddenly felt unbearable ... too dirty, too broken, too forgotten. He looked toward the door as the EMTs entered the room and gently placed the boy onto the gurney under a warm blanket for the trip to the hospital. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax put his hand gently on the boy’s arm. “Let’s get you somewhere clean. Somewhere safe. And I’ll bring your brother to be with you.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As he walked out the door, Jax looked back once. No kid should ever have to survive in a place like that. And no brother should have to steal to keep him alive.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="3_Teach_Your_Children"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">3 - Teach Your Children</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="3_Teach_Your_Children"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Micah_is_OK_and_Calvin_talks_t"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Micah_is_OK_and_Calvin_talks_t"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> hospital room was dim but clean ... a soft beeping from the monitor, the distant murmur of nurses. Micah lay curled beneath a warm blanket on the small hospital bed, an IV taped to his right arm. His eyes were half-open, drowsy from the fluids and protein slowly working their way into his system. His knit cat toy tucked safely in his left arm.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin hovered at the edge of the bed, his hand resting lightly on the blanket. His eyes were rimmed red from crying ... out of relief this time. The boy didn’t speak right away. He just watched his little brother breathe.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood just inside the doorway, arms crossed. He didn’t move forward yet, letting the moment be Calvin’s.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah finally stirred. His eyes found Calvin’s.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A long pause. Then: “Hey,” Calvin whispered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah’s bottom lip trembled. He reached out, slow and shaky. Calvin took his hand.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You came back,” Micah said, barely a breath.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I told you I would.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A tiny sob slipped out of the younger boy. “I thought you got caught. I thought ...”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I did,” Calvin said. “But… the police helped. They believed me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah’s eyes moved to Jax, who gave him a soft nod.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Your brother’s a good kid,” Jax said. “He took care of you as best he could.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked down, taking his brother’s small hand in his. “I’m sorry you were alone for so long.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It’s okay,” Micah said automatically, as if forgiving was a habit. Then: “She’s not coming back, is she?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin hesitated. His mouth opened, then shut. He sat down carefully beside the bed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” he said quietly. “She’s not.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah clutched the cat tighter, burying his fingers in the softness. But he didn’t cry. He just nodded, like some part of him already knew. Then he said, almost mechanically: “She picked the pills again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Calvin said. “She did.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Silence. Neither brother spoke, but the weight of that realization filled the room with dread.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to leave, but Calvin called out. “Wait.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax paused.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked at him, something flickering behind his exhaustion. “What happens to us now?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped forward slowly. “Social Services will help figure that out. Ms. Greer is already working on it. There’ll be interviews, maybe a temporary foster home ... but you’re both together now, and that matters.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s fingers tightened on Micah’s. “Can I stay with him? I don’t care where. Just not apart.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’ll make sure they know that’s your priority,” Jax said. “You’ve earned that ask, and I know Ms. Greer. She’ll want you two together, and she will be trying her best to make that happen for you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah’s eyelids slowly shut again, his small body giving in to rest. Calvin reached for the blanket and gently pulled it up to his brother’s neck.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As Jax turned again to leave, Calvin spoke one more time, trying to sound all grown up. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Can we talk somewhere?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Absolutely.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax led Calvin to the little visitors’ lounge just down the hall. Along the way, Jax got a can of soda from the machine. As they sat in the chairs, Jax handed Calvin the can, and the youngster looked at Jax like he was deciding how much to share.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin sat across from him, hoodie sleeves still pulled over his fists out of habit, even though it was warm here. He stared at the soda on the small table in front of him, like it might give him answers.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Mom started with pills,” Calvin said after a long stretch of silence. “Back pain, she said. From working doubles. At the diner first, then at the hospital laundry.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax listened, eyes steady, not pushing.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She was okay for a while,” Calvin continued. “Tired, but still her. She’d sing to Micah when he was sick. Made grilled cheese with the crusts cut off. She always said she hated hospitals, but she worked there anyway. Said we needed the check more than she needed to feel comfortable.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He looked down, thumb tapping against the cup. “Then she got laid off. And the pills didn’t stop.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded slowly. “That happens to a lot of people. They think they’ll stop after the pain’s gone, but the pain changes.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She stopped singing first,” Calvin said. “Then she stopped cooking. One night, she came home and had forgotten Micah at school. Just forgot. I was twelve.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s voice didn’t shake. He wasn’t crying. He sounded like someone reporting from the ruins after the fire had burned out.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She stopped calling us her boys. She started locking herself in the bedroom for whole weekends. Sometimes, I’d bang on the door, and she’d yell at me like I was some stranger. Or cry. Or not answer at all.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What about your dad?” Jax asked gently.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin gave a tight shrug. “Don’t know. I never met him. She didn’t like talking about him. I think he left when she was pregnant with Micah.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax let that sit. There was too much silence in Calvin’s life already ... he didn’t need Jax to fill it with empty words.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“One night, she left with some guy,” Calvin finally continued. “Said she’d be back in the morning. She left money on the counter. I bought cereal and a can of soup.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He took a sip of the soda, and his lip trembled. “She never came back.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“How long ago was that?” Jax asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Six months.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax exhaled, low and slow. And looked at Calvin ... a kid balancing on the edge between boyhood and battle-worn survivor. He’d seen grown men break under less.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin took a deep breath before continuing. “I told Micah she was working. At a different job. That she had to stay there, and Micah stopped asking after a while.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And you kept things going?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sort of. I used the last of the money for rent until we got behind. Then, I figured out how to keep the heat low so they wouldn’t notice. Learned where to go for leftover food behind the diner. Took small stuff from the stores in the neighborhood.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Why didn’t you tell anyone?” Jax asked, not judging ... genuinely trying to understand.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked up then, eyes sharp with something older than his years.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Because I knew what would happen. They’d split us up. Put us in different homes. Maybe not even in the same city. I’d never find him again. And no one was gonna keep him safe the way I could.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax reached out and took the youngster’s hand. “You did good, Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked away, blinking hard. “It wasn’t all bad. I mean, Micah’s laugh? That kept me going. Some nights, we’d play shadow puppets on the wall ‘til he fell asleep. I’d tell him stories I made up. Just to pretend we were normal. And every once in a while, I’d hear him hum that song sometimes, the one she used to sing.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You were more than normal,” Jax said. “You were everything he needed.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s lips pressed tight. “Sometimes I still think maybe she’ll walk through the door. That maybe she’ll be clean. Say sorry. But then I remember the way she looked that last night. Eyes all sunk in. Hands shaking. She didn’t even kiss him goodbye.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Silence stretched again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The silence stretched the seconds into minutes, but neither seemed to care. Jax leaned back in his chair, watching Calvin stare into the corner of the room. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But… but what if I didn’t do enough?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’ve carried a lot,” Jax finally said. “More than most adults I know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin gave Jax a slight, self-deprecating shrug. “Didn’t have a choice.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Jax agreed. “You didn’t just survive, you protected someone. That’s a choice. The right one. Don’t ever forget that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He waited, watching Calvin digest what he’d said, then said gently: “You want me to try to find her?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin didn’t answer right away. He stared off toward the hallway window like the question had to make its way through layers of scar tissue before he could answer.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” he said finally.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The word landed with finality.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t push. “You sure?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded once. “Even if you did, she wouldn’t come. Not for real. Not stay. And if she did, it wouldn’t be the version Micah remembers.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He looked up then, meeting Jax’s eyes ... his voice steady, but his face tight with old hurt. “We already said goodbye. She just wasn’t there to hear it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded slowly, absorbing that.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I don’t hate her,” Calvin added after a moment. “But I’m not waiting anymore. I’ve got Micah. That’s who matters.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked at the boy sitting across from him ... thirteen, but carrying a life’s worth of weight.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You don’t have to figure all that out today,” Jax said. “Or tomorrow. Just focus on Micah. You’re a good brother.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked down at his hands. “I’m trying.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned forward, elbows on the table.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You did more than try, Calvin. It will be my privilege to work with Ms. Greer to help you both. You don’t have to do this alone anymore. We’ll try to get you boys placed together, or at least close enough you can see each other.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s eyes shimmered with tears for half a second, but he blinked them away fast.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I believe you,” he said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood and helped the youngster from the couch. “Come on. I arranged for you to be on a cot in the same room as Micah. You’re going to need to be rested and ready to make shadow puppets in a few hours.” <text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 4</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="4_Already_Gone"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">4 - Already Gone</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="4_Already_Gone"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_and_Sandy_talk"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_and_Sandy_talk"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> house was quiet when Jax stepped in. Just the soft tick of the clock over the mantel and the faint hum of the fridge breaking the silence. He slipped off his boots at the door, trying not to wake Sandy, but the living room lamp was on low. She was curled on the couch, a blanket over her legs and a book closed in her lap.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked up when he entered. “Hey,” she said softly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Hey.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You eat?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He shook his head. “Not hungry.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy set the book aside, patting the cushion next to her. “You want to talk?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax let out a breath and sank beside her. For a moment, he didn’t say anything. Just stared across the room at nothing. Sandy waited, knowing better than to fill the silence that wasn’t hers.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It was two kids,” he finally said. “Brothers, Calvin and Micah, thirteen and eight. Their mom abandoned them six months ago. Just… left. The older one’s been taking care of the little one ever since. Hiding it. Stealing to survive. Playing dad, mom, and big brother all at once.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy reached over and took his hand. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat, looking out the window at the dark front yard, the clock ticking through its ministrations.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Struggling to regain his composure, he sighed and took a deep breath before continuing.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s a good kid. Better than some of the men I’ve known. Including some in uniform.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy rubbed her hand across his arm. “You did what you could?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” he said. “But it doesn’t feel like enough. Not for them. And not for me either.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy watched him struggle, remembering what they’d learned in counseling ... sometimes silence was the best kind of listening.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He leaned forward, elbows on his knees. “Blumfield… he wanted to book the older one for shoplifting. No questions, no room for the story behind it. Just crime equals criminal. He looked at that boy and saw a threat. I looked at him and saw a survivor.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy remained quiet, letting him continue.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m trying not to hate Blumfield for it,” Jax said. “I know he’s just green and scared and holding on to rules like they’ll save him. But… he didn’t even look at the kid. Not really. He didn’t see him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And you did,” Sandy said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s jaw tightened. “I saw myself. I saw a scared kid trying to protect someone they love. I saw what happens when the system waits too long to care.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy slid her hand to his back. “You made sure they weren’t alone tonight.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They shouldn’t have been alone any night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He scrubbed a hand over his face. “I keep thinking about what Calvin said ... how he made shadow puppets on the wall to help his little brother Micah sleep. And today, when I asked if he wanted me to find his mom… he said no. That they had already said goodbye, she just wasn’t there to hear it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">His voice cracked a little. “How does a kid get that grown up without anyone noticing?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You noticed,” Sandy whispered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded, but it felt like guilt.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And Blumfield?” she asked gently.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shook his head. “He followed the book, not the boy. I don’t know if I can work with someone like that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then maybe you teach him to see. Or you protect those boys anyway and let your actions be the difference.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He looked at her finally. “How do you still believe people can change?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She smiled softly. “Because I married someone who never stops trying to be better. And because kids like Calvin and Micah still exist.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy leaned into his chest, letting her warmth speak the reassurance words couldn’t.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned back, letting the silence settle again ... this time, not so heavily.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy stayed close, her head resting lightly on his shoulder, fingers still curled gently around his hand. Her warmth was reassuring, the kind that didn’t ask anything, just stayed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">After a minute, she spoke again… softly. “By the way… Stan called.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stirred, not lifting his head but opening his eyes, gaze landing somewhere near the coffee table. “Yeah?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy nodded. “Earlier this evening. Said he tried your cell, but it went straight to voicemail.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think I forgot to turn on the ringer,” Jax murmured, barely audible.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I figured.” She let the moment breathe before continuing. “He and Angela want us to come over this weekend. Nothing fancy. Just dinner, some kind of impromptu backyard thing if the weather holds.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t answer right away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He said you sounded tired the last time you talked. He asked how you were doing, and I told him you’d probably roll your eyes at the question but maybe still needed the check-in.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax exhaled through his nose. “That sounds like Stan.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy smiled faintly. “He said Angela’s already planning to make that three-alarm chili you pretend not to like.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He snorted under his breath. “It’s not chili. It’s a chemical weapon.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She said that’s exactly what you’d say. She also said you’re eating two bowls, or she’s sending leftovers home with us in Tupperware.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A small, genuine smile played at Jax’s mouth for half a second. “God help us.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy shifted to see his face. “I told them we’d think about it. I didn’t want to answer for you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked down at their intertwined hands. His thumb moved slightly, running across the side of her index finger.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I don’t know if I’ll be much fun.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You don’t have to be fun,” Sandy said gently. “You just have to be present.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded, neither committing nor brushing it off.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy leaned her head against him again, her voice a sigh. “I think it might help, seeing them. Being somewhere that doesn’t feel so heavy. Just for a little while.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared at the dark window across the room, his reflection faint in the glass, barely there.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep thinking about that kid,” he said. “About both of them. And how easily we almost missed it. If I hadn’t stopped, if I’d just taken Blumfield’s word and walked away…”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But you didn’t,” Sandy said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No. I didn’t.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She squeezed his hand. “That’s why I want you to go see Stan and Angela. Because you can’t carry every kid on your back, Jax ... not alone. You don’t get to save everyone. But the ones you do… they matter. And you can’t forget to come home to the life you’ve got.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded slowly. “Yeah. Maybe.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We don’t have to stay long,” Sandy said. “We’ll make an appearance, eat much too spicy chili, argue about whether Angela’s cornbread is better this time, and then we come home.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Don’t insult the cornbread,” Jax murmured. “That’s how friendships die.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She laughed lightly, the sound soft and clean in the still room.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Outside, the wind rustled through the leaves. Inside, the clock ticked steady and small, counting the time like there was nothing else that mattered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned into the back of the couch and closed his eyes, letting himself believe ... for tonight ... that maybe two brothers would sleep easier. And that maybe, just maybe, there was still space in the world for healing.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">And for chili.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 5</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="5_Handle_With_Care"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">5 - Handle With Care</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="5_Handle_With_Care"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_meets_Lazano"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_meets_Lazano"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> rapped twice on the frosted-glass door, then opened it without waiting.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Captain Holcomb looked up from behind a fortress of paperwork. His glasses were halfway down his nose, and his coffee cup had the remnants of a long-gone cold beside him. “Jax,” he said. “You look like hell.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Cap. I’m going for the gold medal in looking like I lost a fight with a sleepless week.” Jax stepped inside and closed the door behind him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You bringing me more bad news?” Holcomb asked, leaning back in his chair.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stayed standing. “I need to talk about Blumfield.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">That got Holcomb’s full attention. He sat up straighter, folding his hands in front of him. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Alright. Start from the top.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We caught a shoplifting call yesterday. Just a kid at a convenience store. Turned out to be part of something much deeper.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax laid it out ... not the sanitized version for paperwork, but the truth. Calvin. Micah. The abandoned apartment. The shadows on the wall. Blumfield’s insistence on charges and refusal to see anything beyond the shoplifting. Jax kept his tone even, but it was tight. Controlled.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb held up a hand before Jax could continue. “The store clerk filed a complaint this morning. Says she felt ‘pressured and harassed’ by your partner.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s mouth tightened. “He pushed her hard. In the wrong way.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb nodded. “I also have a report from Sergeant Ramirez, and I viewed his body camera footage this morning. Blumfield escalated without cause and showed a lack of discernment.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a slow exhale, some of the tightness in his shoulders uncoiling. “Then I’m not the only one seeing it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb nodded. “No. You’re not. But I’m interested in your assessment. Do you think he was out of line?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He was blind, at least,” Jax said. “And rigid. Not a bad guy ... but he’s clinging to the manual like it’s a parachute. No judgment. No curiosity. Just boxes to check and rules to follow.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re not describing a cop,” Holcomb said. “You’re describing a liability.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax hesitated. “He’s green. But yeah ... if that kid had been cuffed and booked, we’d be having a very different conversation today. One I wouldn’t be able to walk away from.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb nodded slowly. “So, what do you want me to do? Pull him off the street? Transfer him? Boot him?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Jax said. “At least not yet.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb’s brow lifted. “Then what?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m going to suggest you put him on desk duty. Let him shadow someone who actually sees the job for what it is. Davis, or Torres. Someone with more than reflex and ego.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And you?” Holcomb asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax glanced at the stack of old case folders near Holcomb’s elbow. For a second, he thought about the boy’s face in the backseat ... wide-eyed, silent, holding his brother’s hand like a lifeline.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep running cases.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb leaned back, rubbing the bridge of his nose. “I will need to assign you a partner. That’s not a suggestion, it’s department policy.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t argue. That incident still lingered like a bruise beneath the surface ... too many moving parts, not enough support. He gave a reluctant nod.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb opened a drawer, pulled a file, and slid it halfway across the desk. “Harriet Lozano.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax raised an eyebrow at her service record. “Vice is a long walk from Homicide.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She’s got sharp instincts and doesn’t lose her head. She’s been asking to transfer into Robbery Homicide for six months and has specifically asked to work with you. Word is she respects how you work. Might even challenge you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax picked up the folder, flipped it open, and scanned the summary. “She’s blunt,” he noted, reading a supervisor’s comment.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Holcomb said. “And steady. You don’t need another hothead. You need someone who listens before they react.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There was silence as Jax considered it. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb leaned forward, voice low and firm. “Blumfield’s not a lost cause. But he’s also not your responsibility to fix, Jax. You’ve been mentoring rookies for years, but this one… he needs a different kind of correction. This is how we protect the department and the public. And this is how we protect you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb let the silence stretch another second, then added, “And for what it’s worth… this isn’t permanent.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“If all goes well, Nicky seems to be on track to be cleared to return in about 8 or 9 more weeks. That’s the department’s projection based on the reports from therapy, and he’ll need to pass the physical readiness assessment. You’ll be partnered back up then.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a slow nod. He didn’t smile, but something in his jaw eased. “Assuming I haven’t trained Lozano to hate me by then.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She’s sharper than that,” Holcomb said. “She’ll know it’s not personal, and she knows it’s short term.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax closed the folder and handed it back to Holcomb. “Good. Nicky coming back means we did something right. When do I meet her?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She’s in briefing now. I’ll have her at your desk in an hour.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to leave, hand on the doorknob.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Jax?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He paused.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not many guys would come in here and say what you just did. That matters.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax glanced back. “So do those kids.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb nodded. “Yeah. They do.” He paused. “Ask Blumfield to step in. Thank you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped out into the hallway, the door closing with a solid click. He had a new partner now … for at least a few months. And somewhere out there, two boys were in a safer place because someone had paused long enough to see the whole story.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He only hoped the next story would end the same way.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">About an hour later, Jax was nursing his second cup of lukewarm coffee when he saw her coming down the hallway. Mid-thirties. She was slim but carried herself like someone who’d shoved bigger men into walls without apology. Dark eyes that clocked every corner of the room, like she was always looking for the blind spot. No makeup, dark jeans, and a leather jacket over a black blouse. Professional, but not by the book. That tracked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She stopped at his desk without hesitation.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lieutenant Jackson?” she asked, voice low and direct.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He stood and offered a hand. “You must be Detective Lozano.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Harriet,” she said, shaking once. Firm grip. “Or just Lozano. I’m not picky.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded. “Call me Jax. Holcomb told me you were coming.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I figured,” she said. “I’ve read your casework. Impressive. A little unconventional, but it gets results.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax arched a brow. “That’s one way to describe it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She smirked slightly. “Not a dig. It simply means I won’t waste time learning your rhythm. I’m used to guys who take corners wide.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He studied her a beat longer. “Vice is a different world. You sure you want to be in Robbery-Homicide?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Her gaze didn’t waver. “Vice teaches you to listen, dig, lie clean, and stay invisible. Good for surviving. Bad for sleeping. I’m done being invisible. I want the cases that leave a mark.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“That’s a hell of a reason, but the sleep isn’t any easier here.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It’s the truth. And vice keeps you in the shadows. You hear the screams, but you never get to follow them home.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She stopped for a moment. “Too many ghosts,” she added, quieter now. “None of them mine.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded once, then gestured to the empty desk across from him. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As she sat, she removed a small, framed picture from her briefcase and placed it on the desk. A boy, maybe seven, with reddish blond hair like hers, grinning wide with a tooth missing, arms raised in triumph beside a Lego set.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked at it and smiled. “Your son?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. The only good thing that came out of my disaster of a marriage. I get him every other week and …”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stopped and looked away, realizing she had probably said too much.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax waited a moment. “I’m sorry. I didn’t mean to pry. I just believe that partners talk, compare notes, watch the corners, even about the personal stuff.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sure,” she said, already sliding her jacket off and settling in. “I’ve had enough of lone wolves and cowboy cops.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then we’re going to get along just fine.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She pulled a small, well-worn notebook from her valise and opened it. “Tell me what mess we’re wading into first.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Convenience store robbery,” Jax said. He hesitated, just long enough to remember the boy’s eyes. “The clerk stole the till and blamed a thirteen-year-old kid. It might’ve been a cover for something else. Still working the angles.” He studied her expression. No blink, no frown ... just readiness. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The case number is RHD-16-3164827. Familiarize yourself with it while I finish up the paperwork on the kid.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And your last partner?” she asked, carefully neutral.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Blumfield’s reassigned. Good kid, wrong fit.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She didn’t press. Just nodded. “Alright. Let’s see where this one goes.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As she settled in, Jax caught a faint scent of gun oil and peppermint ... a strange mix. She was different from Nicky and light years from Blumfield, but that could be a good thing. He took a sip of cold coffee and set it down. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lozano?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“One more thing,” Jax said, his voice low. “I don’t need backup. I need a partner.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano didn’t flinch. “Then you’ve got one.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax busied himself with typing in his interaction with Calvin and Micah, including a note to see if they could find the mother and charge her with child abandonment.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano skimmed the file with practiced eyes, flipping through the incident report, preliminary statements, and the clerk’s complaint. She didn’t speak for a while ... just wrote a series of lines into her notebook. Jax watched her out of the corner of his eye. She twirled her pen when she read. A habit, he thought, or maybe just a way to pace her mind. He didn’t know if he trusted her yet. But he didn’t </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">not</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"> trust her either. That was something.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, she looked up. “You believed the kid?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I believed something was off,” Jax said. “Too clean. The clerk called it in like she had a script ready. Security footage only shows Calvin taking food. Nothing from behind the counter. And then you see her putting cash from the till into her purse.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She nodded once. “And the brother?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Found him holed up in an abandoned apartment. No food. No adult in sight. Just a kid trying to keep his little brother alive.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano tapped the file. “Then why wasn’t the clerk pressed harder?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Blumfield took the complaint at face value,” Jax said. “Didn’t ask questions that didn’t already have a box to check.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano sat back, eyes narrowing. “So the clerk skimmed from the till and pinned it on the kid.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yep.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano flipped the case file back a page. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The clerk claims she called it in five minutes after the kid ran, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The video confirms that, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Absolutely.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then we go talk to her.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax tilted his head slightly, a bit impressed at her sharp analysis. “That eager?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She shrugged. “If this clerk’s lying, she won’t sit with that lie for long. Not now that someone’s looking.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He watched her a beat, weighing the energy behind her calm. Steady, like Holcomb said. No rush, no bravado. Just clarity.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Alright,” he said. “Let’s see what she does when the story doesn’t hold.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano closed the folder. “You want me to take the lead?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax hesitated ... not from doubt, but from the unfamiliar sensation of trust trying to resurface. He’d always been the one to go first. But this time… maybe it was better to watch how she played it. Perhaps not leading was the more brilliant move … just this once.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Let’s go back and press her on the timeline,” Lozano said. “Let’s see what happens when someone notices her script doesn’t match the stage directions.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They stood at the same time, both reaching for jackets without speaking. Lozano slipped hers on, and Jax threw his coat over his shoulder. It wasn’t a rhythm yet ... but it could be.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As they headed toward the elevator, Jax glanced at her. “You always come in this sharp?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano gave a small, almost imperceptible smile. “Only when someone’s about to lie to me … That’s when I start listening closest.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. Holcomb had called it ... she’d challenge him. Maybe that wasn’t such a bad thing. Perhaps it was exactly what the job needed. What he needed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 6</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="6_Badlands"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">6 - Badlands</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="6_Badlands"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Interview_the_clerk"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Interview_the_clerk"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> interrogation room was bright but cold, the kind of municipal white that turned skin sallow. The clerk, Marla Krantz, sat at the table in a wrinkled polo and stained khakis, jaw tight, eyes hard. She crossed her arms like they were armor.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano entered first, the case file tucked under her arm. No fanfare. Jax followed and leaned against the wall, silent and unreadable.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Ms. Krantz? I’m Detective Lozano, and this is my partner, Lieutenant Jackson.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano set the folder on the table but didn’t open it. She took a seat, sat back like she had all day. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She said nothing, letting the silence stretch until the tick of the clock’s second hand was louder than breath. Fifteen minutes passed before Marla finally flinched.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You going to ask me something?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Have you ever done community theater, Ms. Krantz?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla blinked. “What?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Acting. Script work. Blocking. You know ... rehearsed performance.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla scowled. “No.” Marla Krantz - clerk</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Just curious. The way you gave your statement ... it was clean. Rehearsed. Down to the minute. Almost like you practiced it before the officers walked in.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I told the officer what happened,” Marla said, her voice clipped. “Kid came in, started looking around real twitchy, grabbed some stuff, ran out. I checked the drawer, saw I was short, and I called it in.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano nodded. Made a small note. “Right. Said you noticed the shortage after he ran. Called it in at 7:32 p.m.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yep.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We pulled the security footage,” Lozano said, still calm. “Shows the kid leaving at 7:29. You opened the register at 7:31 and slipped something into your purse. Then you picked up the phone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla’s mouth tightened. “I don’t remember every second. I work at the register all day.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano didn’t flinch. “You took $214 … the exact amount that came up short when your drawer was counted. And this isn’t the first time you’ve skimmed. Two past incidents flagged in the system ... both borderline, just under the amount that would force a report.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax watched the woman’s face closely. He could see it in her eyes. The flicker of nerves beneath the surface. But she wasn’t cracking.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Instead, Marla leaned forward. “So now I’m the criminal? Some little hood rat comes in, probably already juvie-bound, and I’m the bad guy?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano didn’t answer right away. She let the words hang, untouched.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally: “You’re the adult. And you made the call.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I knew what he was,” Marla snapped. “Don’t act like you don’t see it, too. Hoodie up, eyes darting. Mouth shut like he’s got something to hide. You let kids like that run loose, and they grow into something worse.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shifted. His mouth opened, then closed. Let Lozano drive.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You know what he was doing?” Lozano asked. “Trying to keep his little brother from starving. The kid didn’t have food. No adults. Just an abandoned apartment and a handful of cheap groceries.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla rolled her eyes. “Oh, please! You people fall for that sob story every time. I don’t care if he’s feeding a dozen siblings ... he stole. That’s the end of it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano leaned forward slightly, voice quiet. “He didn’t take money. You did. And you blamed him because you thought no one would look past your word.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla sneered. “You think anyone’s going to cry over one more thief-in-training getting processed? That kid’s probably already got a record. Don’t waste your sympathy.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano studied her for a long time. “He doesn’t. Not a single charge. Never even had a truancy report.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Marla didn’t respond. Her face remained locked in the same defensive scowl, but her eyes darted once… not with guilt, just calculation.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m not giving the money back,” she said, suddenly. “You can forget that. I earned every cent of it by putting up with that store and those hours. You think they care about me?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stood up. “We’ll let the DA sort it out. Theft, false police report, obstruction.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“That’s crap. I made a mistake, and now you want to ruin me?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You made a decision,” Lozano said. “And the only person you ruined is a scared thirteen-year-old trying to survive.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Krantz’s voice rose, shrill. “He’s not scared. He’s a liar. All of them are.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">That was it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stood and pulled her handcuffs from the leather case on her belt.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Interview’s over. Stand up,” she said, voice cool but firm. “Marla Krantz, you’re under arrest for theft, filing a false report, and obstruction.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Krantz started to protest, but Lozano had already stepped behind her, guiding her up with practiced efficiency.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Stand up. Hands behind your back.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She clicked the cuffs on without ceremony, then recited the Miranda warning, steady as ever.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You have the right to remain silent. Anything you say can and will be used against you in a court of law…”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The pair escorted her to the booking area and then returned to their desks to begin filling out the paperwork.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She wasn’t scared of the charges,” Lozano said. “She was scared of being wrong.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She wasn’t scared at all,” Jax replied. “She hated that kid the second he walked in the door. Everything after that was justifying it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano exhaled, steadying herself. “People like her ... they don’t see children. They see shadows.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You handled it clean,” Jax said. “If it were me, I might’ve gone for sympathy. Might’ve tried to get her to see the kid’s side.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She didn’t want to see it,” Lozano said. “Some people don’t need a second chance. They need a record.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded slowly. “You were right to take the lead.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked over at him. “Still think you don’t need a partner?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave her a half-smile, grim and quiet. “I think I need the right one.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The pair diligently worked on the reports. The coffee had gone cold. The protein bar sat half-open, forgotten between them. Everything paused—except for the fluorescent lights humming overhead, steady and indifferent.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano was finishing her report on Krantz, typing steadily.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat with one leg propped against his desk drawer, staring at the charge sheet. His pen tapped against the margin, distracted. He hadn’t realized how tightly his jaw was set until the phone rang.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">His cell. Not the desk line.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He glanced down.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Children’s Medical Center.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">His thumb hovered over the answer button a second longer than it should have. He picked up. “Lieutenant Jackson.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A pause.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then the voice came, low and quick. A nurse. He didn’t catch her name.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lieutenant ... I’m calling about Micah Spence. You’re listed on the contact sheet as emergency fallback.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax straightened. “I’m leading the investigation that Micah is part of.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“There’s been a change. He’s spiked a fever. Blood pressure’s crashing. We’ve moved him to the PICU.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Is he conscious?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not currently. We’ve sedated him until we stabilize him. But you should come.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’ll be there in fifteen.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He hung up without another word.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano looked up. “What’s wrong?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax was already on his feet and grabbing his coat. “Micah. He’s crashing. They’re moving him to intensive care.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stood. “Go. I’ll finish the report and close out the booking. Just text me if you need backup.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax was halfway to the door before she finished speaking.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 7</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="7_Behind_Blue_Eyes"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">7 - Behind Blue Eyes</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="7_Behind_Blue_Eyes"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_goes_to_visit_Micah_in_PIC"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_goes_to_visit_Micah_in_PIC"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> hallway was too quiet. The kind of quiet that made Jax feel like he was arriving too late.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He stepped off the elevator, shoulders tense, ears straining for some sign of life ... a voice, a footstep, anything. A nurse met him a few moments later, clipboard in hand, expression calm but tired.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s stable for now,” she said. “But it was close. Malnutrition has weakened his immune response; any minor infection could escalate rapidly. We’ve got him on fluids, antibiotics, and a warming blanket. We’ll keep monitoring through the night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded, jaw tight. “Can I see him?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Just for a few minutes. You’ll need this mask.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She led him down the hall, past the hum of machines and muted voices. Micah’s room was dim, with monitors glowing softly in green and amber. The boy was small against the wide bed, an IV in each arm, oxygen in his nose. A thin sheen of sweat coated his skin.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood just inside the door. He didn’t move closer right away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, he stepped in, pulling a chair over. He sat down slowly, the chair legs squeaking faintly on the tile.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You scared me, kid,” Jax said softly. “Not allowed. Not like this.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah didn’t respond. Just breathed ... shallow, even, distant.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked at his little hand resting on the blanket. The IV tape crinkled.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">No answer. Just the beeping and the soft whoosh of air from the oxygen line.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’ve got more people in your corner than you think, kid. So don’t check out yet.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He didn’t cry. He didn’t move. Just sat there, hand on the rail, watching the rise and fall of the boy’s chest like it was the only thing keeping the world in place.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_and_Calvin_talk_about_what"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_and_Calvin_talk_about_what"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> walls were the same off-white as every other waiting room in the city. The chairs were stiff, bolted down, angled toward a muted TV no one watched. A vending machine hummed near the corner, full of things no one needed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:s/>Jax had called the precinct first, asking Lozano to bring Calvin to the hospital. Then he called Sandy. She’d made a simple dinner for them, then put it away when he didn’t come home. She was used to it by now ... not that Jax ever wanted her to be.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">About ten minutes later, Lozano and Calvin came into the waiting room, the boy obviously scared of the place and the circumstances. Jax tried to get him to sit and relax, but when Calvin finally sat, he sat on the edge of his chair, elbows on his knees, fingers locked tight. His backpack was on the floor beside him ... a habit more than a necessity now. Like if he kept it close, nothing else could fall apart.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood nearby, arms crossed, silent. He didn’t want to say it. But the nurse said Calvin needed to be told.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“His fever came out of nowhere,” Jax said. “They think it’s a complication from the malnutrition. He’s in the ICU. They’ve stabilized him for now, but he’s not conscious.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded slowly. Swallowed hard. His eyes were red, but dry.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I should’ve gotten him better food,” the teen said, voice rough. “Or found more medicine when he had that cough. Or figured something else out. Anything.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You did everything you could,” Jax said quietly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No!” Calvin snapped. “I hid him. He slept on that floor. I should’ve done better.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You are thirteen, Calvin. A kid.” Jax said. “And you kept him alive when no one else was going to.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked at the floor, jaw clenched. The tears started to stream down his face.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat down in the chair next to him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He didn’t answer, staring at the floor like he was trying to hold the whole room still with his eyes.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin? Look at me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The teen finally looked up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Micah is fighting. Hard. And he’s got a lot of people helping him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, Calvin managed to whisper, “Can I see him?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Soon,” Jax said. “They just want to make sure he’s stable for the night. I will try to get you in first.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded, but the stillness in him cracked. He leaned forward, hands to his face, and let out a sound too quiet to be called crying ... but it carried just as much weight.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked at his partner and nodded towards the door. “Lozano, why don’t you call it a night? I’ll sit with him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano sighed. “Sure, Jax. Call me when you need me to come back.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t say anything more. He didn’t offer clichés or promises he couldn’t keep. He just stayed there … close … steady.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat like that for a long time.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Sandy_brings_dinner_to_the_hos"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Sandy_brings_dinner_to_the_hos"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> waiting room was quiet except for the hum of the vending machine. A nurse walked past without stopping, and the glow of the overhead lights had softened to something dull and clinical. The TV in the corner cycled through late-night ads for walk-in bathtubs and miracle pans.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat slouched in a stiff plastic chair, legs stretched out, arms crossed. Calvin was curled on the row of seats beside him, hood up, backpack still clutched close. He hadn’t spoken in a while, hadn’t moved much either.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The door opened with a soft hiss. Jax looked up, expecting a nurse. It was Sandy, dressed in her jacket, shoulders dusted faintly with cold air from the outside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She didn’t say anything right away. Just looked at Calvin, trying to decide if the boy was awake or not.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Didn’t think hospital vending machines were going to cut it,” she said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax blinked. “You didn’t have to ...”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I know,” she said. “That’s why I did.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She walked over and set three takeout containers on the low table near the wall. Chinese takeout ... still warm, the edges of the containers fogged from steam. “How is Micah doing?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s holding steady,” Jax said. “They’ve got him sedated.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy gave a short nod. Her eyes flicked to Calvin.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin sleeping?” she asked softly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A voice answered before Jax could.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Calvin said, his face still turned toward the wall. Not loud. Just firm. Clear.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy didn’t flinch.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She crouched slightly, angling her voice toward him. “Okay. Good. I brought food. Nothing heavy. Just rice, chicken, something warm.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin shifted slightly, sitting up more upright. He didn’t look at her directly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a nod between them. “Calvin, my wife, Sandy. Sandy, Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You hungry?” she asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A pause. Then Calvin finally answered: “Yeah.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax reached for the containers, handing one over to Calvin before opening his own.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy sat next to her husband, took the last container of food, and started eating as well.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked up. “You didn’t have to.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She gave a slight shrug. “Neither did you, when you listened to him the first time.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There was silence as Jax and Sandy dug into their food, but Calvin just held the one he’d been handed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks,” Calvin said, barely above a whisper, eyes still low. But the grip on his food container tightened like it mattered. His hands were trembling now, but he didn’t stop. He took the first bite, like someone who didn’t know if he deserved it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy nodded at the youngster. “We’re here, Calvin. For real. No one’s bailing tonight.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The three ate in silence, side by side ... not talking, but not alone. Not tonight.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Somewhere in the night, Calvin’s fork scraped softly against the bottom of the container.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Micah_is_OK"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">Morning</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Micah_is_OK"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> came slowly, seeping into the corners of the waiting room with the kind of pale, indifferent light that didn’t care who had or hadn’t slept. The overhead fluorescents still buzzed faintly, but now their glow had to compete with the dull gray of dawn pressing against the windows.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin was asleep, slouched deep into the corner of a chair, one arm crooked over his face, the other wrapped around his backpack like it was the only thing that mattered to him. The food container from the night before sat forgotten on the table in front of him, lid askew, fork resting atop it like the remnants of a battle survived.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy had dozed off against Jax’s side sometime around three. Her coat had slipped from her shoulder, and her hand was still lightly looped through his arm, fingers curled into the fabric of his sleeve. She breathed slowly, steadily, curled close like gravity had pulled her toward him in sleep.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax hadn’t slept. His body was stiff, joints aching from the hours hunched in a too-hard chair, but he didn’t shift. The discomfort was background noise. His focus was on what to tell Calvin if Micah didn’t make it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The hallway was empty now, the chaos of the night replaced by the slow rhythm of the hospital waking up. He could hear the distant clink of a cart, the low murmur of voices somewhere far down the corridor. A new day was starting. The door opened with a soft click.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned his head, expecting another passing nurse. But this one stepped in <text:s/>... late 30s maybe, with her hair in a tight bun and a lanyard clipped to her pocket. She had a cup of coffee in one hand, a tablet in the other, and the kind of posture that said she’d already been on her feet for hours.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She paused just inside the door when she saw them, then offered a nod of recognition.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re here for Micah Spence?” she asked, voice quiet and even.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded once, pulled out his badge, and showed it to the nurse. “Yeah. I’m the lead investigator on the case and his emergency contact.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She took a few steps forward, glancing down at the sleeping forms beside him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nodding to the sleeping teen. “His brother, Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then toward Sandy. “And my wife.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The nurse gave a slight nod, checked something on her screen, then met Jax’s gaze again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He made it through the night. Fever broke around three. He’s still very weak, but stable. We’re tapering the sedation now, slow and careful.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s shoulders lowered a fraction. “That’s good.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It is,” she said. “He’ll need long-term care. Things like nutritional support, physical therapy, possibly speech and occupational rehab, depending on how he responds. But for now, we’re on the right side of the line.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took a slow breath and nodded. “Is he awake?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not yet. But his brain activity is stronger. Reflexes are improving. He might start registering voices soon.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She paused.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“One visitor at a time. You can go in if you like.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked past her toward the hallway. Then back. “Let his brother go in first.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The nurse shook her head no. “People under eighteen are not allowed.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Miss, don’t make me go get a court order. Calvin’s done more to keep that young boy alive in the last six months than you want to know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The nurse started to say something, but the look on Jax’s face was enough to convince her otherwise. She nodded and said, “Okay. He’ll need to leave the backpack here, put on scrubs and a mask.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “No problem. I’ll tell him. Give us a bit to get Calvin up, and I’ll send him to find you. Thank you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re welcome.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The boy was stirring now, shifting under the blanket Sandy had draped over him sometime in the night. He blinked up at the ceiling, brow furrowed, then turned his head slightly when he saw Jax watching.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s awake?” Calvin asked, voice thick with sleep and fear.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not quite,” Jax said. “But he made it through the night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin sat up straighter, rubbing at his eyes with the heel of his palm. “Can I… see him?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “Yeah. That’s what I was gonna ask you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin blinked, surprised. “You’re not going in first?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shook his head. “You’re his brother. You’ve earned it more than anyone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s face twisted like he was trying to hold back something sharp in his chest. He swallowed hard, then nodded. “Okay.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You need to leave the backpack here. The nurse will get you some clean scrubs and a mask to keep Micah safe.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy stirred beside Jax, sleep still clinging to her. “Everything alright?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Jax said gently. “Micah’s stable. Calvin’s gonna sit with him for a bit.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She nodded, rubbing her eyes, then gave Calvin a small smile. “Go on, kiddo.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin stood slowly, hesitated. “Do I have to say anything? Or?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Doesn’t matter,” Jax said. “Just be there. He’ll know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin slung his backpack over his shoulder like a reflex, then gently set it down instead. He wiped his hands on his jeans, took a breath, and went to find the nurse.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax watched as the boy disappeared down the hall, his steps quiet but steady. He leaned back in his chair, finally closing his eyes. For the first time all night, there was a little space to breathe.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 8</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="8_Dont_Let_It_Bring_You_Down"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">8 - Don&apos;t Let It Bring You Down</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="8_Dont_Let_It_Bring_You_Down"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Calivin_is_in_with_Micah_Jax_a"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Calivin_is_in_with_Micah_Jax_a"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> sat with both hands wrapped around a cup of coffee. It wasn’t hot anymore, but she held it like warmth was still a possibility. The waiting room had mostly emptied. A few early-morning visitors shuffled in and out, but the air still felt hushed, like it hadn’t caught up with the sun.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood near the window, arms crossed, eyes unfocused. He hadn’t said much since Calvin had gone into Micah’s room.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“How long has he been in there?” Sandy asked softly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax glanced at the clock. “Almost forty minutes.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She nodded. “That’s good, though. He needed it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax made a low sound in his throat. Agreement, maybe. Or something heavier.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy watched him from over the rim of her cup. “You thinking about what’s next?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m thinking about what it took for him to keep them alive this long.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She didn’t interrupt.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s thirteen, Sandy,” Jax said. “Thirteen. I was learning how to pitch a curveball at that age. He’s been stealing food and keeping fevers down with cold rags.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Some kids don’t get the luxury of childhood,” she said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He didn’t just lose it,” Jax said. “It got yanked out from under him. His mom disappears, and nobody notices. He keeps his brother alive, keeps them hidden for six months, and the only time anyone sees him is when he screws up trying to get dinner.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy reached over and put a hand on his arm. “You saw him. That counts.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax let out a long breath and sank into the chair beside her.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep thinking ... what if we hadn’t answered that call? What if it had gone to someone else? Someone who didn’t take a second look at a stolen loaf of bread and a bruised banana?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You did take a second look,” she said. “And now Micah’s in a hospital bed instead of a body bag. That’s not nothing.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax was quiet for a long moment.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He didn’t even want help. He wanted to protect his brother, take the blame, and get back to hiding.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Because that’s what he’s used to,” Sandy said gently. “When you’re thirteen and there are no adults around, you don’t have room for trust. Just survival.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked over at her. “You think he can come back from this?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think he already started,” she said. “The second he handed over that backpack. The second he let Micah go in that ambulance.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded. “He’s gonna need people. People who give a damn. People who don’t just show up when there’s paperwork to file.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then let’s be those people,” Sandy said. “It’s not too late.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat in silence for a few moments, gazing out into the hallway. Jax rested his elbows on his knees, head bowed slightly. Sandy leaned gently against him, her hand still on his arm.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s just a kid,” Jax murmured. “A stubborn, scared, brave little kid.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy leaned back slightly, the coffee cup now resting on her knee. She glanced toward the hallway, then back at Jax.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What about the mother?” she asked. “Are you going to try to find her?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax rubbed a hand over his jaw, eyes fixed on some point far off. “We already ran her name. Didn’t get much.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But you’re still looking?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He hesitated.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sorry, I’ll have to turn it over to Missing Persons,” he said finally. “Let them do a deeper dig. But I’m not holding my breath.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy studied him. “You think she’s dead?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think she left,” Jax said, voice flat. “And didn’t look back. No calls. No drop-ins. No CPS reports. Just two kids off the grid for six months. That doesn’t happen unless someone wants to stay gone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy frowned. “She could’ve been in trouble. Or scared.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Maybe,” he said. “But scared doesn’t mean you leave your kids in an empty apartment with no food.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She didn’t argue. Just looked down at the rim of her cup, her expression troubled.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“So what happens now?” she asked quietly. “To them. To Calvin and Micah.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax exhaled and leaned back in the chair, tilting his head against the wall.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“If Micah pulls through and stabilizes, they’ll both go into the system. Separate placements, probably.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Separate?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Depends on the judge and the caseworker,” he said. “But Micah’s going to need a medical foster home ... someone trained to deal with kids coming out of the hospital. Calvin’s still a minor, but he’s too old for most family placements. Odds are, he ends up in a group home.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy’s face tightened. “That’s a hell of a reward for keeping your brother alive.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Jax said. “It is.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“There has to be someone,” Sandy said. “No cousins? Grandparents? Family friends? Anyone?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shook his head. “If there is, Calvin doesn’t trust them. And no one’s come forward. Six months, and not a single missing person’s report was filed.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat in silence for a moment. The hum of vending machines and fluorescent lights filled the space where hope should’ve been.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy looked over at him again. “Is there… anything we can do?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t answer right away. His eyes flicked toward the hallway where Calvin had walked. Then he let out a breath and nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m going to work closely with Ms. Greer from Children’s Services,” he said. “Make sure Calvin doesn’t get tossed into whatever bed they have open. Try to make sure Micah’s recovery isn’t an excuse to separate them for good. If they are placed separately, at least get them placed close so Calvin can go see Micah.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy’s expression softened. “She’s the one who came to the precinct that night, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Jax said. “Smart. Tough. Doesn’t talk down to Calvin. I think he might listen to her. That’s something.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And she’ll listen to you?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“She will,” Jax said. “Or she’ll learn to.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy gave a small smile. “Good.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat for a moment longer, the air between them a little lighter, even if the road ahead wasn’t.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Those boys have been on their own long enough,” Sandy said firmly. “It’s time someone stood up for them.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t argue. This time, he just nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy glanced at her watch and made a face. “I’ve got to go.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to her. “Work?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. District Headquarters,” she said, standing slowly and stretching her shoulders. “We’re still trying to get the third-grade curriculum finalized for next year. Reading comprehension benchmarks, math sequencing… and, my favorite, politics.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sounds like a blast.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She gave him a tired smirk. “It’s not glamorous, but it matters. These kids need more than just catch-up programs and outdated lesson plans. Half of them are already behind before they even walk in the door.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood with her. “You sure you’re okay driving? I can get you a ride.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m good.” She squeezed his hand briefly. “You’ll text me if anything changes?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. Of course.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy grabbed her coat from the back of the chair and slipped it on, then glanced toward the hallway.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Tell Calvin I’ll check in later. And if Micah wakes up …”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’ll let him know you care.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She paused, then added, “Those boys matter, Jax. You know that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I do.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She leaned down and kissed his cheek, then walked out with that quiet purpose she always carried ... the kind that didn’t need to make noise to leave an impact.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax watched the door close behind her, then turned back to the waiting room, where the silence had settled in again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Still a long road ahead. But for once, they weren’t walking it alone.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_reports_to_the_Captain_abo"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_reports_to_the_Captain_abo"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> waiting room was empty now. Just rows of stiff chairs, a humming vending machine, and the soft flicker of early-morning light leaking through the high windows. Even the TV in the corner had been turned off, as if the building knew it was too early to pretend optimism.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat forward in his chair, elbows on his knees, phone in hand. He stared at the screen for a moment before scrolling to Holcomb’s number and hitting the dial button.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Two rings.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Holcomb.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It’s Jackson.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Where are you?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Still at Children’s Medical Center. PICU.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A brief pause.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The six-year-old, Micah, made it through the night. Fever broke around three. He’s stable now, sedated. They’ve got him on fluids and antibiotics. Kid’s hanging on, but he’s wrecked, Cap. Six months of neglect, maybe more.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb’s voice dropped a shade. “And the older one?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Calvin. He’s with him now. First time he’s let go of that backpack since we picked him up.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There was a pause. The kind that Holcomb only gave when something was hitting harder than he wanted to admit.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You think the mother’s dead?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think she walked. There were no signs of violence in the apartment, nor was there any sign of a struggle. Just… gone. No phone calls, no medical records, no attempts to check in with anyone. I’ll send it to Missing Persons, but I don’t think she wants to be found.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You keeping the case?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Jax said. “At least for now. I’m coordinating with Ms. Greer from Children’s Services. She’s solid. I’m gonna push to keep the boys together, or at least make sure Calvin doesn’t get buried in the system.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb exhaled slowly. “That’ll be a fight.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. Worth having, though.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Another pause. A faint click on the other end ... Holcomb with his pen, no doubt pacing in that cramped office behind a stack of folders.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You sleeping at all?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Not yet.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Get some before you turn into a liability. Get patrol to drive you home.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave the ghost of a laugh. “Working on it, boss.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There was a moment of silence. Then Holcomb’s voice came back, softer now.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You did good, Jax. It could’ve gone a different way. A lot of guys would’ve booked the kid and moved on.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Didn’t feel right.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It wasn’t,” Holcomb said. “Keep me posted. Take the time you need.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Will do.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax ended the call and let the phone rest on his knee. The silence in the room returned ... thick, still, unbroken. Somewhere down the hall, a door opened and closed. A nurse’s voice murmured something low. Then quiet again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned back in the chair, eyes on the ceiling. Not asleep. But not ready to move yet, either. Just waiting.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Calvin_is_back_they_go_for_bre"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Calvin_is_back_they_go_for_bre"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> door opened with a soft hiss. Jax stirred in his chair, the sound just enough to rouse him from the edge of sleep. He blinked, sat up straighter, and rubbed his eyes. The phone still rested on his thigh, the waiting room still empty except for the low hum of machines and the faint buzz of the overhead lights.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin stood in the doorway, back in his clothes, the scrubs folded neatly under one arm. His hair was mussed, his eyes red-rimmed, but there was something different in his face. Softer. Lighter.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sat forward. “Hey.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded once. “He woke up. Just for a second.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax straightened, fully alert now. “Yeah?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s voice cracked around the edges, but he kept it together. “He looked at me. Smiled. Didn’t say anything, but he saw me. I know he did.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s chest eased. He hadn’t realized how tightly he’d been holding himself until that moment.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“That’s good,” he smiled. “That’s really good.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin stepped inside and dropped into the chair beside Jax. He didn’t say anything for a while. Just sat there, slouched deep, the way kids did when the weight finally started to lift ... not all the way, but enough to breathe.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax waited, giving him space. Eventually, Calvin spoke again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I didn’t think he’d make it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You got him this far,” Jax said. “You kept him alive. You didn’t quit.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin shook his head slowly. “I almost did.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But you didn’t.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Another pause. Then Calvin whispered, “He smiled like… like he wasn’t mad at me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned slightly in his seat to face him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s not,” he said. “He’s your little brother. All he saw was the person who never left.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin wiped his sleeve across his eyes, then tucked his arms across his chest. “I don’t know what’s gonna happen to us.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Me neither,” Jax said honestly. “But I’ll be here while we figure it out.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin didn’t answer. But for the first time since they brought him in, he leaned sideways, just slightly, until his shoulder touched Jax’s.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He didn’t pull away. And Jax didn’t move.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat in silence for a few more minutes, the stillness between them no longer heavy.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then Jax gave a slight stretch and pushed himself up from the chair.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Come on,” he said, nudging Calvin lightly with the back of his hand. “Let’s go get some food.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked up, bleary-eyed. “They have a cafeteria?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah. It’s not great,” Jax said, grabbing his jacket from the back of the chair. “But it’s edible. Mostly. If you avoid the meatloaf.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin stood slowly, rubbing at one eye with the back of his hand. “Do they have pancakes?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Since it’s past seven, probably. If not, we’ll find you a chocolate chip muffin and call it a win.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin gave a tired, crooked grin. “Okay.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They headed for the elevators, the hum of early hospital activity starting to pick up around them ... carts squeaking down hallways, phones ringing faintly at nurses’ stations, the world turning on.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stopped by the nurse’s station momentarily to let them know where they were going in case there were any developments.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pressed the down button and glanced over at the boy beside him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You did good in there, Calvin. I’m proud of you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin didn’t say anything right away. But his chin lifted slightly. And when the elevator doors opened, he stepped inside with just a little more weight off his shoulders.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 9</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="9_With_A_Little_Help_From_My_F"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">9 - With A Little Help From My Friends</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="9_With_A_Little_Help_From_My_F"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_Sandy_eat_with_Nicky_and_A"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_Sandy_eat_with_Nicky_and_A"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> backyard smelled like summer and slow-burn spices. Jax and Sandy stepped through the open side gate and into warm air and low light. String lights stretched across the patio, already glowing as the sun dipped behind the trees. Angela was at the grill, flipping burgers beside a bubbling pot of chili perched on the side burner.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“There they are!” she called. “Right on time. You brought your stomachs, I hope.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy smiled and moved in for a hug. “Smells like fire and regret.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Angela grinned. “Perfect. Then it’s working.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky stood nearby with a beer in hand and his crutch resting against the patio railing. The worst of the limp had faded, but the rhythm of his movements was still off, like a drummer relearning an old beat with a slightly different grip. The gunshot had torn through his upper thigh, close enough to nick the femoral bundle and keep the trauma team on high alert that first night. For a while, the question hadn’t been when he’d get back on his feet, but if.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s supposed to be taking it easy,” Angela said, gesturing toward Nicky with the grill tongs, “but you’d think he’s training for a decathlon.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m just trying to keep the leg from locking up,” Nicky said, easing down into one of the patio chairs. He moved with care—subtle, measured—but Jax caught the twitch in his jaw as he sat. Pain, still there, still sharp some days. “My PT says progress is a straight line if you squint and lie to yourself.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Angela scoffed. “Your PT says you need to stop skipping the foam roller and lying about your pain scale.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy chuckled. “Sounds about right.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The nerve pain’s been the worst,” Nicky admitted, flexing his foot slightly. “Sometimes it lights up out of nowhere, like someone flipped a switch under my skin. And stairs still suck. I’ve learned to respect good knees.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took a beer from the cooler and cracked the top. “But you’re walking.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m walking,” Nicky said, with a nod. “I can’t run, can’t drive more than twenty minutes without needing to stretch, and I definitely can’t chase a suspect down a back alley yet—but yeah. I’m walking. And I’ll take that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Angela ladled chili into mismatched bowls. “He’s underselling it. He fought like crazy to get here. One surgery. A month on crutches. Six weeks of broken sleep, maybe two hours at a time. But he kept moving.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Mostly to avoid Brent,” Nicky muttered. “The physical therapist from hell.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Angela rolled her eyes at her husband. “Oh, come on, Brent isn’t that bad.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sweety, Brent smiles while making me do one-legged squats like he is training me for some kind of sadist circus act.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy snorted, Jax coughed mid-sip, and both barely kept their beers in their mouths and not spraying all over the table.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Don’t forget the mood swings,” Angela added, handing Sandy a bowl of chili. “I got to live with those. Pain meds, frustration, feeling useless … he was charming.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I was delightful,” Nicky said, deadpan.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You tried to mop the kitchen with a cane,” Angela replied. “While on oxycodone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax laughed, but his eyes lingered on Nicky a beat longer. He remembered the early days—the first visit to the hospital, Nicky pale and groggy, lines in his arms and worry in his wife’s eyes. Then the rehab center, where grit met gravity every day, and pride had to take a back seat to healing. Now Nicky looked leaner, not just from muscle loss, but from the way the ordeal had stripped him down to something elemental. Still sharp, still standing, and still willing to get back in the fight.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They all sat at the table—burgers, chili, cornbread, under a checkered towel. For a while, it was just casual chatter, shared laughter, and stories that meandered and overlapped. Jax mostly listened. The chili still brought tears to his eyes, but the company made it easier to bear.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Eventually, the sun dipped low, and the bugs came out. The women moved inside with dishes in hand, promising coffee and something sweet for dessert.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Angela paused before ducking through the sliding door. “Try not to get stuck in your heads out here,” she said, eyeing the two men. “No brooding over the beer.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We make no promises,” Nicky said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They both sat back in their chairs, beers in hand, the quiet settling in around them like an old coat.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">For a while, neither spoke. Crickets chirped from the edge of the yard. A siren whined faintly in the distance and faded out.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then Nicky said, “You read the report again?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took a sip before answering. “Yeah. Says we shouldn’t have split up. ‘Breakdown in field coordination.’” He paused. “‘Supervisory lapse.’”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky let out a breath. “Feels like they needed someone to blame.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They always do,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep thinking,” Nicky said, “if I’d stuck with you … if we hadn’t peeled off … I don’t get shot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded, slow. “Maybe. Or maybe we both get tagged. Or maybe nothing happens at all.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But it wasn’t the suspect who hit me,” Nicky said. “It was our own guy.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“A rookie,” Jax said. “Supposed to hold the line. He panicked and didn’t ID. Didn’t wait. Just shot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky took a long pull from his beer. “And the report makes it sound like we fumbled a play, not that some kid with a badge fired on a partner.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They don’t want it on the academy,” Jax shrugged. “And they sure as hell don’t want it on the department’s head. So they stack the language until it falls on us.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Us?” Nicky probed. “You’re thinking it fell on you?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax glanced at him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They called it a lapse in senior field command,” Nicky said. “And since you have more seniority than me, you think that’s on you? ”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t respond right away. Just leaned back and let the silence fill the space between them.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep wondering,” Jax said finally, “if I should’ve stopped you. Pulled you back. Called the move.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We moved how we always move,” Nicky said. “Instinct. Split the alley. Close the gap.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And the rhythm cost us.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Nicky said. “The rhythm got hijacked by a kid who never should’ve stayed where I put him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked down at his thumb, slowly peeling the label from the empty beer bottle. “Doesn’t change the outcome.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Nicky agreed. “But it changes the story.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They sat in quiet again. The lights above them buzzed faintly. From inside, the clink of dishes and the low murmur of the women’s conversation drifted through the screen door.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood and retrieved two more cold beers from the cooler, handing one to Nicky.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then Jax said, “Holcomb told me something this week.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky raised an eyebrow.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“When you’re finished therapy and pass the physical readiness assessment, they’re putting us back together. Same detail. Same rhythm.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A slow grin crept across Nicky’s face. “Seriously?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax smiled and nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Damn,” Nicky said. He leaned back in his chair. “I didn’t think they’d have the nerve.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Neither did I.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You good with it?” Nicky asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took another drink. “I am.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky nodded. “Then I’m in. But this time, no improvising. We call every move.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Agreed.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And I’m not chasing anyone with a head start.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax smirked. “You never were.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The stillness took over for a few moments, easier now.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then Nicky said, “You still working on the Dixon case?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a slow nod. “Yeah. Still spinning on one thing.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky raised an eyebrow.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The shooter left the rifle,” Jax said. “Just dropped the rifle on the floor and disappeared.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Any prints?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“None on the gun, none on the brass.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky frowned. “Nothing?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No smudges, no partials, no DNA.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Cameras?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed. “Not working on the back side of the building. They were out of commission due to some sort of power issue. And the cameras from our side showed no one leaving.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He planned it … every inch. Just slipped out the back before we had it sealed.” Nicky nodded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took a sip of his beer. “No witnesses either. At least none that came forward.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky shook his head. “Pretty typical for that neighborhood. No one wants to be involved, so they keep quiet.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared out toward the fence.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It’s not just about solving the case anymore,” he finally said. “It’s about proving he can’t just disappear in our city. That he doesn’t get to walk away clean.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">From inside, Sandy’s voice called through the screen door, something about coffee. Angela laughed, and the sound of it softened the edges of the night.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood, slower now, joints stiff from stillness.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We’ll find him,” he said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Nicky pushed up with his crutch. “Next time, we finish it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They headed toward the house where laughter still lived, and light waited, and stepped inside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Together.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 10</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="10_Back_In_The_Saddle"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">10 - Back In The Saddle</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="10_Back_In_The_Saddle"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="The_rifle_has_a_history_in_CSU"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="The_rifle_has_a_history_in_CSU"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> rifle had a history.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax flipped through the lab report as he and Lozano stepped off the elevator and into the CSU offices, tucked into the basement level of the precinct. The air smelled faintly of coffee and ethanol, and the hum of refrigeration units underscored the quiet.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The report was three pages, stapled at the corner, and full of jargon, but the key line stood out in bold under the technician&apos;s summary:</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ballistics match a 2010 cold case: East Olympia, two victims, unsolved.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank Becker met them just inside the ballistic lab. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax met the man with a nod. “Frank?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank held up his copy of the file like it weighed more than it should.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Took a while,” he said. “We ran it through every system we’ve got … state, federal, internal. Nothing until this morning. Some archived evidence from a closed precinct finally got digitized.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“East Olympia?” Jax asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank nodded. “Two victims shot execution style in an empty parking lot. No witnesses, no clear motive. The case went cold fast. The rifle was never found. Until now.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano frowned, scanning the report again. “So it sits quiet for six years, then resurfaces in a downtown sniper shooting?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Or was it kept quiet?” Frank said. “Moved through the wrong hands, never meant to show up again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax scanned the report. “This East Olympia incident looks too similar, Frank.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I thought so, as well. The person who took those shots knew what they were doing. Cameras down, clean brass and no witnesses.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Maybe a proof-of-concept run?” Lozano speculated.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t like how that sounded right.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank handed over a fresh printout. “I can have records pull the original Olympia file if you want the full write-up.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Do it, and send it up to us, please,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Frank, we’ll catch up later.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The pair left CSU and headed back to the elevator.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s phone buzzed—text from the hospital. “Micah is stable. Calvin asked if you were coming back.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared at the screen for a long moment. “When it rains…”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano looked at Jax. “What?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed. “Hospital again. Micah is doing better and Calvin wants to see me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano put her hand on Jax’s arm. “The boy is just worried about what’s going to happen to his brother and him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “I know. I put a call into Ms. Greer when I first got here, and I’m waiting to hear any news.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She smiled. “Why don’t we stop by after lunch?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And if we don’t have news?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The boys deserve to know.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed. “You’re right.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As they got off the elevator on the fifth floor, they went and stood outside Captain Holcomb’s office. The door was cracked, but the blinds were drawn. Jax rapped once and pushed the door open.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb looked up from a cluttered desk, his sleeves rolled and expression unreadable. “Back already?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We’ve got something,” Jax said, stepping inside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano followed, offering the report Frank had given them. Holcomb took it, scanned the summary page, and set it down with a sigh. “The rifle used on Dixon and the Chief was used before.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “Back in 2010. Double homicide. East Olympia. The case went cold. Same gun, same clean escape, same lack of evidence.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano folded her arms. “This changes the profile. Whoever pulled the trigger has done it before, and left no trace either time.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb gave a slow nod, but didn’t speak. His jaw worked behind the silence.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax waited, then said, “We need to keep working this.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb’s eyes lifted to him. “You’ve been close to this case since day one. Too close, maybe. Dixon was yours. The Chief nearly died. You watched it happen. And now you want to stay on it?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m already on it,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:s/>He hesitated for a moment. “This isn’t about Evan anymore. It’s about a shooter who knows how to disappear. And a rifle that keeps coming back.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb exhaled slowly. “Command’s watching this case like it’s radioactive. And IA hasn’t stopped sniffing since the rookie shot Nicky. You walk into that fire, you take the heat with you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m not asking to go in alone,” Jax said. “Lozano takes point. My name’s not on the front of any report unless you say so.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb looked at Lozano. “You good with that?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She nodded. “He’s not chasing ghosts. He’s tracking a pattern.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb was quiet for a long beat. Then he tapped the file with his knuckle. “Fine. But every move goes through me. You keep it tight. No improvising.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We understand,” Lozano said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a sharp nod. “Appreciate it, Cap.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb waved them off, already pulling up a file on his screen. “Close the door on your way out.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:s/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Revisit_the_scene_of_the_shoot"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Revisit_the_scene_of_the_shoot"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> building was quiet now. No yellow tape, no uniforms posted out front. Just the faded hush of a crime that had already moved on.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped out of the elevator with Lozano at his side. The air on the top floor still smelled of dust and cheap paint. He led her to the stairwell and up out into the afternoon air.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The rooftop was as empty as Jax remembered. No wind, just the hollow quiet that came after something violent. A piece of yellow tape fluttered, forgotten, at the base of a rusted pipe. The city stretched around them, unconcerned.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stepped out behind him, scanning the space with that sideways-cop head tilt—cataloging lines of sight, exit points, cover. She knelt by where the rifle had been found, dropped on the ground next to the HVAC unit. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked around and studied the area. “You sure this is where they left it?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Right there,” Jax said. “CSU bagged it within the hour.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No prints, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Right. Like the shooter had worn gloves, or wiped it down.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And no witnesses, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No one we talked to… But maybe someone saw more than they knew.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She nodded and looked around for another few minutes. “I think he wanted the rifle found.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax tilted his head. “Explain.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“In Vice,” she said, “we used to bust stash handoffs where runners would leave drugs hidden in crawl spaces or HVAC units. The people who retrieved it weren’t the ones who stashed it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He raised an eyebrow. “You think the rifle was dropped here deliberately?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She shrugged. “I think maybe someone carried it up, used it, dropped it here, and someone else was supposed to pick it up after. But maybe that second someone bailed, or panicked.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood, his head swirling with possibilities.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano walked over to the HVAC unit and pointed. “That vent cover was loose when they found it, right?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “Yeah. CSU thought it might have been loose for some time. Looked rusted where the latch is.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Or someone was waiting in there for the handoff.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">That stopped Jax for a second.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“An HVAC unit as a blind drop,” he said aloud. “Shooter ditches the rifle, someone else was supposed to be in the unit, grab it and go.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“But they didn’t,” Lozano finished.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood, pulse ticking. Something about it rang too neat. Could it be? Could all of them have missed something so obvious?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A silence fell between them. The city buzzed far below, but here, it was just wind, wire, and memory.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped closer to the ledge, his eyes tracing the rooftops beyond. It came automatically to him … scanning alley angles, fire escapes, shadows that leaned too wrong.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then it came again. Soft. Confident. The voice whispered right behind his ear.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Look left.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stiffened.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano didn’t notice. She was still looking at the HVAC unit, trying to see if there were any marks on the inside.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">But Jax’s eyes had already turned to the left.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">And there, down two rooftops, half-hidden by a rooftop vent, was a mounted camera on a hardware store’s awning. It pointed at the alley below. The blind spot no one had accounted for.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Hold that thought,” he said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano followed his gaze. “You see something?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax was already pulling out his phone. “Camera. Two rooftops east. Might’ve caught a piece of the timeline we’re missing.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano grinned. “Now you’re thinking like Vice.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t smile. He just stared at the distant camera and gave a quiet nod.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” he said. “But I’m not sure I like where it’s taking me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P27" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 11</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="11_Shelter_From_the_Storm"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">11 - Shelter From the Storm</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="11_Shelter_From_the_Storm"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Revisit_Calvin"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Revisit_Calvin"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> elevator chimed softly on the pediatric floor of Children’s Medical Center, and the doors slid open with a practiced hush. Jax stepped out first, still in his coat, a paper bag from a corner cafe tucked under one arm — cookies, juice boxes, and a wrapped sandwich he hadn’t decided if he’d give away or eat himself. Lozano followed, scanning the floor with quiet vigilance, even here, where everything smelled like disinfectant and crayons.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“This won’t take long,” Jax said, not really looking back.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No rush,” Lozano replied. “I wouldn’t mind seeing how they’re doing.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They passed a toddler wheeling an IV pole decorated with glitter stickers, and a nurse humming to herself while updating a chart. Room 517 was halfway down the hall. The door cracked just enough to reveal a sliver of soft light and quiet breathing. Jax tapped gently.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Inside, Calvin sat beside Micah’s bed, one arm resting lightly across the younger boy’s legs. His head was tilted against the back of the chair, chin tucked, half-asleep. Micah lay curled beneath a cartoon blanket, IV still taped to his left arm, but his breathing was steady and deeper now. His face had color. The hospital bear was still clearly on duty, tightly held in Micah’s right arm.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax eased the door open farther and stepped in. “Hey,” he said softly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s eyes blinked open. He straightened quickly, reflexively tense, until recognition kicked in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You remember my partner,” Jax added.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano gave a small wave. “Hey, Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded. “Hi.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax crossed the room and set the bag down on the window ledge. “Brought you something. Cookies and juice. Might be against hospital policy, so hide the evidence quick.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin gave a faint, grateful smile. “Thanks.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah stirred at the sound, his eyes fluttering open. He looked from Jax to Lozano, then to Calvin.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin leaned in. “Hey. You’re okay.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah blinked at him, then whispered, “They let you stay?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded. “Yeah. I’m not going anywhere.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah’s eyes softened, his voice barely audible. “Good. I don’t like it when I can’t hear you breathe.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin blinked hard and looked down, gently wrapping his hand around Micah’s. He didn’t speak, but the silence between them said everything.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stood at the foot of the bed, watching with quiet care. No pity. Just presence. Jax watched as Calvin split a cookie in half and handed it to Micah, as if it were a treasure. The boy took a bite and sank back into the pillow, thumb absently stroking the bear’s warm fur.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There was something sacred about the stillness that settled in that small hospital room. The fluorescent lights hummed, monitors blinked their steady rhythms, and for a few rare minutes, time didn’t seem to move forward at all.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">After a few minutes, Jax stood and rested a hand on Calvin’s shoulder. “You’re doing good. He’s in the right place now. So are you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin nodded, but his voice was quiet. “Do you know if they’re keeping us together? Like… in the same place?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax crouched slightly, meeting his eyes. “I’ve got a meeting with Ms. Greer in the morning. She’s been working hard on the case, and once we know more, we’ll walk you through what comes next.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s expression didn’t change. “But?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t sugarcoat it. “Foster placements don’t happen overnight, Calvin. It’s a process. Paperwork, court orders, and availability. But I promise you this … I’ll keep pushing. Every meeting, every report, I’ll make sure they know being together is your desire.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin’s face tightened — not in anger, but in wary hope. “You really think they’ll listen?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I do,” Jax said. “Because they’ll be hearing it from more than one voice. Mine. Greer’s. And yours. You don’t give up on your brother. We’re not giving up on you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Micah shifted under the blanket, his small hand still wrapped around Calvin’s wrist. A deep breath. A quiet sigh. Then stillness again as the young boy drifted off to sleep.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked down at him. “Thanks,” he said, voice nearly a whisper.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave his shoulder a gentle squeeze. “Get some rest. You’re not carrying this alone anymore.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They stepped into the hallway, the door gently shutting behind them. Jax lingered at the small window cut into the frame, watching Calvin lean back into the chair, exhaustion catching up with him at last. Micah’s hand stayed locked around his wrist like a tether.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He’s stronger than I thought,” Lozano said eventually.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax exhaled — a long, quiet breath. Not dramatic. Just the release of something clenched too long. “They both are.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They stood there a moment longer in silence.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano’s voice dropped low. “That clerk. Marla Krantz. You think she’s ever going to feel anything about what she did?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax considered the question. “I think people like her lie to themselves better than they lie to us.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano nodded. “I guess.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They passed the nurses’ station and turned the corner toward the elevators. The hum of the hospital faded behind them — monitors, low murmurs, the small everyday work of keeping people alive.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Wonder why I stayed a cop so long?” Jax asked, thumb brushing the elevator button.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No,” Lozano said. “But I figured it wasn’t for the coffee.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A faint smile touched his mouth. “It was something like this. Someone worth showing up for. Even if it’s only once in a while.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The elevator doors opened. They stepped in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Let’s get this video from that hardware store over to CSU.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano nodded. “And pray we got lucky.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The lab should be able to stabilize the angle, punch in on the alley. If we see a guy, we’ll know soon.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And if not?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Then we go back to the top,” Jax said. “Start again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The elevator opened on the first floor. They crossed the lobby side by side, past a waiting room with sleeping toddlers and vending machines humming like engines. The automatic doors whooshed open, and the night air met them with a slap of wind. Dusk had thickened. Streetlights buzzed to life, casting long, uneven shadows across the parking lot.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They climbed into the car without another word. Lozano tossed her jacket over the back seat. Jax started the engine and let it idle while he stared out across the street.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As Lozano buckled her seat belt, she quietly asked. “You ever feel like all of this… the system, the paperwork, the call logs… like it’s built to miss kids like them?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“All the time.” He put the car in gear. “We just have to work hard to make sure we don’t.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They pulled away from the curb, the hospital fading behind them. The road ahead opened up, and traffic was light. They now had a new lead, but a long way to go.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">But for one night, two boys were warm, safe, and no longer alone.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P28" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 12</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="12_Fade_To_Black"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">12 - Fade To Black</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="12_Fade_To_Black"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Untitled_Section-1"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Untitled_Section-1"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> was halfway through his first cup of coffee and a stale granola bar when his phone lit up with CSU’s number.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He quickly answered. “Jackson.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lieutenant,” came the voice of Frank Becker, the senior tech. “Got something. Can you and Lozano come down after roll call?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s pulse ticked up. “You cleaned up the footage?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We pulled every ounce we could from that hardware store feed. Punched through glare, sharpened contrast, and layered it with thermal ghosting from the rear lens. You’re going to want to see it for yourself.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t hesitate. “We’ll be there.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The lab was dim, lit mostly by monitors and the soft wash of daylight fighting through tinted glass. Frank sat in front of a curved screen, multiple video feeds paused mid-frame. His fingers danced across the keyboard as Jax and Lozano stepped in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Morning,” Frank said. “Didn’t want to send you stills. It’s better when you see it in motion.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano leaned on the back of a chair. “What are we looking at?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank tapped a few keys. “This is the southeast alley camera. We synced it to the rooftop timestamp from the original shooting window. Now watch here.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The video played in stuttered, grainy motion. Shadows danced across the brick alley. At first, it looked like nothing … just silence and stillness.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then, at the 12:58 p.m. mark, something moved.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A blur in the upper left corner. A figure — fast, deliberate — darting from the side exit of the building, hugging the wall, accidentally brushing a dumpster, then disappearing into a shadow.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank paused the footage. Zoomed. The pixels swam, stabilized, and snapped into place. It wasn’t crisp, but it was clear enough now to make out:</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A male silhouette. Tall. Slim. No face was visible from this angle. But definitely a person.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Camera didn’t catch him going in,” Frank said. “But we’ve got him coming out. Fifteen seconds after the shot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax crossed his arms. “Direction?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“North, toward Sycamore.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano said, “Any other cameras at that angle?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank shook his head. “Nothing city-owned. But there’s a pawnshop across the street with a rig on the roof. If you can get them to hand it over …”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We’ll get it, and any others that direction,” Jax said. “Let’s see that again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank let the first video loop again — the tall figure sprinting from the side exit, moving fast.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Stride’s clean,” Frank said. “No limp. Pretty athletic. Covers ground fast. Watch the posture — low center of gravity, controlled movement. He’s done this before.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “Professionals don’t panic. They plan exits.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano muttered, “Until he clips the dumpster.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pointed at the screen. “I wonder. I don’t remember a dumpster there last night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano nodded, “You’re right, neither do I.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax thought for a moment. “Maybe it got put there after he scouted the route and removed after the shooting. Anything else, Frank?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Keep watching after the tall guy disappears.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ten seconds after the first figure vanished, a second person entered the frame. A much smaller, lighter person, carrying a backpack and wearing a hoodie drawn tight around their head. The figure emerged at the alley’s far end, moved slowly past and out of the frame to the south.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano leaned forward. “That’s not the same guy.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Exactly,” Frank said. “Shorter by several inches, maybe eighty pounds lighter. And—watch the gait.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He played the clip on a loop. The second figure’s left foot dragged slightly. Not much. Just enough to knock their balance off rhythm.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“There’s a limp,” Lozano said. “Left side.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah,” Frank confirmed. “Consistent through the walk cycle. Doesn’t look fresh — like it’s lived in.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax studied the screen. “You think they saw the perp?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank shrugged. “My opinion? Yes. But the second person could be a lookout.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They don’t run,” Lozano said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Nope. They walk into the alley, hesitate behind the crates, then head out of frame, down the alley toward Tyne Street. No rush. Almost like they knew what they were stepping into.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared at the still frame Frank had frozen the video on. A slighter silhouette pausing mid-step, posture half-turned, as if they were listening for something.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What is the time difference between the two figures?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Eleven seconds,” Frank said. “Exactly. First guy clears the side exit and vanishes. Eleven seconds later, the second figure walks in from the north and vanishes to the south.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano asked, “Any way to tell gender?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank shook his head. “Too much distortion. The hoodie and baggy clothes kill any chance of body-shape ID. But posture says teenager to me. Could be male or female. But they’re cautious. Like they’re not supposed to be there — but they’re drawn to these events, anyway.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“The backpack? Can you enhance that for us?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sure, Jax. It will take me a couple of hours.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks. Let us know when it’s ready, and send us both clips and stills,” Jax said. “Every version you’ve got, please.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Already done.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Frank. I owe you more tofu tikka masala.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano sighed. “Back to the scene again, and look for videos from the time period?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “And you need to report what we found to the captain before we go.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She smiled at him. “And you have your meeting with Ms. Greer.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Untitled_Section-2"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Untitled_Section-2"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> knocked lightly on the open door of the small conference room tucked just down the hall from the precinct bullpen. Ms. Greer was already inside having come from children’s services, sleeves rolled up, tapping at her laptop with tired precision. Two coffee cups sat beside her — one half-drained, the other forgotten.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked up. “Lieutenant Jackson. Thanks for coming.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He stepped in and closed the door behind him. “You said you had an update.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Greer sighed, brushing a hand across her forehead. “I wish I had something better to give you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pulled out the chair across from her and sat. “Tell me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’ve been on the phone for days. Checked every foster agency in the tri-county area, plus two medical care facilities that sometimes coordinate with us. The answer’s the same at every stop.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked down at the screen, as if expecting an update, then looked up. “We don’t have a home right now that can take both Calvin and Micah together.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s jaw tightened. “Because of Micah’s condition.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Exactly. He’s six years old, with chronic respiratory issues, recent signs of neglect-related malnutrition, and a PICU stay less than a week old. That’s a high medical rating. Most homes aren’t cleared for that.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What about placing Calvin with him at the hospital until …”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I asked,” she interrupted gently. “The hospital won’t allow it. And once Micah stabilizes, they’ll want him discharged.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned forward, elbows on the table. “What about extended family? Anyone?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“None that we’ve been able to reach. And from what Calvin shared, their family tree doesn’t exactly have many branches.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He rubbed his hands together. “So, what are the options?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’ve put in for an emergency exception for a joint therapeutic placement — one with the medical certification for Micah and the capacity to supervise a teen. But it’s a long shot. And even if it’s approved, we may be looking at a wait of several days.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t speak right away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“They’ve been through enough,” he finally said. “You saw them together. Calvin’s the reason that kid is still alive.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Greer’s face softened. “I know. He’s a remarkable boy. Protective, grounded — and right now, he’s the only stable thing in Micah’s world.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Separating them isn’t an option.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I agree,” she said. “But the system isn’t built for this. It’s overloaded. Specialized homes fill up fast, and even the ones that want to help get handcuffed by policy.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Tell me what you need,” Jax said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She looked up. “A strong voice … preferably in uniform … willing to testify to the emotional harm of splitting them up. If we escalate this to family court, I’ll need that kind of statement.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’ll have it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Greer nodded slowly. “I’ll keep checking placements. Twice an hour if I have to. In the meantime, I’m going to request that Micah be held at the hospital as long as medically necessary. Every extra day buys us time, and we probably have a week, but I think we can find him a placement by then.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And Calvin?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m going to have to place him somewhere soon. Probably the day after tomorrow at most.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shook his head. “I don’t like that. If we split them, even temporarily, it’ll hit him hard. He won’t say it, but he’s hanging on by a thread.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I know.” Greer exhaled. “They’re more bonded than most kids I’ve seen. More than siblings. They survived something no one should have to.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“What other options do we have?” Jax asked.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Greer thought for a moment. “I don’t know. Maybe if we could place Calvin somewhere where he’s close to Micah, at least until I can find a more permanent solution.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stood. “Call me the second you have movement. I’ll do the same.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As he reached the door, Greer added, “I’m glad you’re in their corner, Lieutenant.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He paused, one hand on the knob. “You too.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then he stepped out, the door easing shut behind him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P29" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 13</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="13_Suspicious_Minds"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">13 - Suspicious Minds</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="13_Suspicious_Minds"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Untitled_Section-3"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Untitled_Section-3"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> pawn shop on 92nd looked like it hadn’t changed in twenty years—grimy windows cluttered with used electronics and old guitars, the air inside thick with dust and forgotten transactions. Jax stepped through the door first, Lozano just behind him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A bell overhead jingled. The man behind the counter barely looked up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Artie?” Jax asked, flipping open his badge.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lieutenant. Another social call?” the man scoffed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Hardly, Artie. This is my partner, Detective Lozano.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:s/>Artie wiped his hands on a rag, nodding toward the monitors behind the counter. “Some guy from your precinct called earlier. They said you might want to look at our camera footage from the alley. I queued it up.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He turned one of the screens toward them. “The timestamp is from six months ago—same night that Dixon was shot, right? You’re still working that case?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a brief nod. “Yep. New evidence puts the alley in play.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Well, let’s have a look.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Artie hit play.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The grainy footage showed the mouth of the alley</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">. At 7:33:12 p.m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">., a figure emerged—fast, low to the ground. He hesitated at the corner, glancing back once before booking it toward the street.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano crossed her arms. “Still no clear face shot.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Artie ejected the thumb drive and handed it over. “I’ve got another angle from the east wall. Picks him up again just before he hits the corner at 92nd.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Let’s see it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Arte inserted the second thumb drive and hit play. The figure was quick, only in view for a few seconds.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed. “No clear view of his face.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Artie ejected the second drive and handed it over as well. “Sorry, Jax. I wish it showed more.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Artie. It’s more than we had, and I appreciate it. I’ll put in a good word with the D.A. for you. I hope it helps and you can stay open.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Lieutenant. You’ve always been straight with me.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The pair walked down the alley toward the corner.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Car wash across the street might have coverage,” Lozano said. “That alley exit’s just a block from their entrance.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Donut shop too,” Jax added. “Camera above the neon sign, if it’s still working.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And we need to circle back to the south and see if we can get a view of the second person leaving the alley as well.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Untitled_Section-4"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Untitled_Section-4"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> car wash sat catty-corner from the pawnshop, its faded awning and cracked cement lot bathed in the dull glow of flickering lights. It was one of those older drive-through models with soap-streaked walls and a coin dispenser that hadn’t worked in years.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A lanky teenager in a reflective vest looked up from where he was hosing down the sidewalk. Jax flashed his badge.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Lieutenant Jackson and my partner, Detective Lozano. We’re following up on a six-month-old shooting. Wondering if your exterior footage goes back that far.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The kid squinted at him, unbothered. “We got archives on the cloud. I don’t have the login, but my manager’s inside. Hang on.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He disappeared into a side office. Lozano crossed her arms. “You’d think with a shooter on the loose for six months, someone would’ve reviewed these angles already.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax shook his head. “The investigators thought they had the full timeline. Remember, the rifle was found on the roof. Witness statements didn’t yield anything. There was never a visual showing how the shooter left the scene. Now we do.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The kid came back, trailing a middle-aged woman in a grease-streaked polo.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re lucky,” she said. “Most of our cameras crap out in the rain, but that week was dry. Show me the time?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Between</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12"> 7:30 and 7:35 p.m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">.,” Jax said. “Night of the Dixon shooting. We’re looking for a male in a gray hoodie. Came out of the alley behind the pawn shop, crossed here.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She guided them to a cramped back room, punching keys with practiced impatience. The footage loaded.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">At </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">7:33:47,</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"> the suspect appeared—smaller now, further from the camera, but still recognizable. He crossed the lot diagonally, careful not to step into the light. The grain wasn’t good enough for a facial ID, but this was the same guy from the pawn shop angle. Same run-like-you-mean-it gait.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then something caught Lozano’s eye.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Pause. Go back two frames. Right there—see that?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The man passed under a flickering canopy bulb, and for a second, his profile caught the light. Still blurry, but distinct enough: close-cropped hair, prominent nose, angular jaw.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax exhaled through his nose. “That’s our runner. He didn’t just drop the rifle and vanish. He planned his exit.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The manager saved the clip to a USB. “You want the angle from the vacuum station, too? It’s on the corner, and might have caught him as he turned.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Please,” Lozano said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ten minutes later, they had both files in hand and were back outside. The breeze had cooled even further, carrying the scent of fryer oil from down the block.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax pointed with his chin. “Donut shop’s next.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They crossed again. The place was still open, a sleepy mom-and-pop operation. Fluorescent lighting gave everything inside a pale glow. A man in his sixties stood behind the counter, wiping trays.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You two look like you need caffeine more than pastries,” he said, eyeing the badges.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We’re chasing someone,” Lozano replied. “From six months ago. You still have surveillance archives?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Goes back a year. My daughter handles the uploads. What day and time?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax handed over the timeline.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Ten minutes later, they were in the back room again—this one smelled like cinnamon sugar and powdered glaze. The monitor showed the sidewalk out front. Jax leaned in.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There. A</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">t 7:34:11 p.m</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">., the runner passed directly in front of the window. This time, he was closer, and there was a partial side profile. Still no clear ID, but enough detail to build a map of movement.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then came the moment that made Jax’s stomach flip. The man looked up—just for a split second—and glanced toward the donut shop window. Straight at the camera. A faint smirk. Not scared. Just focused. Then gone again.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax rewound it three times, watching that glance. That face. It seemed familiar, but it was just blurry enough that he wasn’t able to tell for sure. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Ok, thank you, sir. We appreciate the videos.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax took the jump drives into his pocket, stepped out into the street, and looked in the direction of the man’s travel. There were no other businesses for several blocks past the car wash, so he simply disappeared into the neighborhood of houses. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:s/>Lozano’s voice got him focused again. “Let’s get the CSU techs to enhance these clips. We might get partial facial markers from that donut shop frame. In the meantime, let’s go look for cameras that might have recorded our second person.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded slowly. “He knew where the cameras were. But not all of them.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Untitled_Section-5"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">They</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Untitled_Section-5"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> returned to the area behind the building where the man had run into the dumpster. And started searching to the south, looking for cameras which would have picked up the second person.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They followed the alley south until it opened onto Tyne Street. Across the intersection, Jax spotted the faded mural of oranges and olives. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">His face broke into a slow smile. “Edie’s Deli. She’ll have cameras. And real coffee.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano nodded. “That would be great. I need the caffeine.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The deli was a squat brick building with a faded mural of oranges and olives on one wall. Inside, the air smelled like pickles and fresh, warm bread. A tiny bell jingled as they stepped through the door.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Edie, behind the counter in her usual stained apron and purple bandana, looked up and grinned. “Well, well. If it isn’t my favorite homicide lieutenant. Or is it robbery this time?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave her a half-smile. “Need a favor, Edie. And some coffee.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You always do,” Edie laughed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He gestured toward the front of the store. “Your exterior cam still running?” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Always, Jax. You know that, or you wouldn’t have asked. Black coffee for you, as always? And your girlfriend here?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Detective Lozano, I’m his partner, and a little cream, please.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Edie, we’re looking for video from six months ago when there was the shooting around the corner. We think that there was a witness who came down that alley from that old hardware store, across Tyne Street, and came this way.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Alvin!”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yes, Grandma?” The boy, not much older than Calvin, emerged from the kitchen area, his hands covered in dish soap. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Can you go pull the video from the day of that shooting, starting at </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">7 p.m.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">, and get it ready for the Lieutenant and his partner to view?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yes, Grandma!”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Sometimes it’s a good thing Alvin keeps everything.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax smiled. He was very familiar with Alvin, a pack rat in the making for anything to do with electronics or video.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Can I interest you two in some fresh pecan pie to go with that coffee?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yes, please. I’ll take a half slice.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano grinned. “I’ll take the other half.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Edie scoffed. “Half slices? What am I, a diner?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“No, ma’am. Just not that hungry.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Ma’am? What are you trying to do, make me feel like an old lady? Call me Edie, everyone does.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano laughed, and the pair dug into the small pieces of pie. Full of nuts, not too sweet, with a light flaky crust. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Alvin reappeared. “I’m ready when you two are.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">They finished their pies and took their coffee cups with them to Edie’s office, where Alvin had the video queued up.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Thanks, Alvin. Hit play, please.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The camera showed the sidewalk outside the deli, angled just enough to catch across the street and half a block in either direction.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“There,” Jax said, pointing.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A figure ran past, southbound. Hoodie drawn tight. Backpack. Small frame. Quick steps. The timestamp: </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">7:35 p.m.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano leaned closer. “Back it up and pause it where you see the face.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Alvin tapped the computer keys and went forward slowly, pausing where the face was clearly visible.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The figure was partially turned, caught mid-stride in the frame. The backpack dangling from the plastic strap, far too small for the person. The hoodie was zipped to the chin, but the camera had captured his face clearly. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed, his shoulders shrinking. “Calvin Spence.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano folded her arms. “So he didn’t tell us everything.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax gave a slow nod, but his eyes didn’t move from the screen. “No… he didn’t.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A long silence settled between them, heavier than before.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Then Jax added, almost to himself, “The question is—why not?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P30" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 14</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="14_Dream_On"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">14 - Dream On</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="14_Dream_On"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Jax_has_a_bad_dream_about_the_"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Jax_has_a_bad_dream_about_the_"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> bedroom was quiet except for the distant rush of wind through the trees. Moonlight spilled in thin lines across the floor, broken by the swaying branches outside. Jax lay on his side, one arm curled under the pillow, the other reaching instinctively across the empty space beside him.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy was in the kitchen, getting a glass of water. She always slept lightly when he was like this — worn to the bone, but never truly at rest.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax twitched.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">His brow tightened.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The dream morphed again. He was back in the building again, leading officers to the right as Nicky led some to the left. Jax stopped where he could see the stairway to the roof of the building in front of him. A pause, then that awful noise.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A gunshot echoed in the dream space, too close, too loud. The crack of the round fractured open the sky. Nicky’s leg buckled. Jax couldn’t reach him. Couldn’t see the shooter. Couldn’t move fast enough. His feet were like anchors. Time bled around the edges, thick and sluggish.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Now he was in the alley, looking for clues. The dumpster had a fresh mark where the shooter had accidentally bumped it. No sound, no hope, no trace of the man.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He yelled into the darkness. “I tried to stop him! I didn’t do enough!”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">A voice echoed in the distance, like it was sometime in next week. Soft and familiar.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Me too.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax turned to the voice, and Calvin emerged from the shadows of the alley. Not dressed in his ragged hoodie, but in hospital scrubs.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re still looking,” the boy said.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I didn’t catch him,” Jax replied.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You were never supposed to.” Calvin stepped forward. “But you saw me. That’s what matters.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax blinked. “What if it’s not enough?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Calvin looked past him. “Then keep looking. Just don’t forget who needs you now.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax tried to speak again, but the dream peeled away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He woke with a jolt. His breath came in short bursts. Sweat clung to his spine. The shadows in the corners of the room looked sharper than they should have.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy stood in the doorway, a glass of water in hand. She’d heard the breath hitch in his sleep. She always did.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Same dream?” she asked softly.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He shook his head, rubbing his eyes. “Worse. I was chasing him this time.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy came to the bed and handed him the glass. He drank, hands shaking, then set the glass on the nightstand.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I think I was getting closer,” he said. “But I never caught him. And when I gave up… Calvin was there.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Closer. But never enough.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She sat beside him, hand on his back.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He told me I was never supposed to catch the guy. Just… see him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“And you did,” Sandy said. “You saw Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah.” He exhaled. “But I don’t know if that’s what he needs. Or if I can do more.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She leaned into him. “You already did what no one else did. You looked close enough to see the difference between a threat and a scared kid.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I keep replaying everything,” he said. “The shooting. Evan’s case. Calvin. Micah. I close my eyes, and it’s like they all take turns.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy rested her head against his shoulder. “You carry a lot, Jax. But you don’t have to carry it alone.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He looked at her. “I’m trying. Some nights, it just catches up.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">She reached for his hand and linked her fingers with his. “Try to stay here, just for now. You’re not on that roof. You’re not in that alley.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He nodded. Not fully convinced. But grateful.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">As they lay back down, Jax stared at the ceiling, listening to her steady breathing beside him. He wasn’t sure if sleep would return. But at least now, when the dream came back, he’d remember Calvin’s voice.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Just don’t forget who needs you now.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23" loext:marker-style-name="T9"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Sandy_and_David_decide_to_take"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Sandy_and_David_decide_to_take"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> morning light was pale and unforgiving, filtered through blinds that hadn’t been opened yet. Jax sat at the kitchen table, staring at a half-eaten slice of toast like it was evidence. His coffee had gone cold.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy moved quietly around the kitchen. No rush, no chatter. She knew that kind of silence, the kind that wasn’t ready to be broken just yet.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, she slid into the seat across from him. “You didn’t go back to sleep.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked at her, got up, dumped the cold coffee in the sink, and got a fresh refill before returning to the table. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“It’s not just the dream. It’s what’s next. For Calvin.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy folded her hands around her mug. “What does the social worker say?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed. “Ms. Greer said they’re struggling to find a placement that can take both boys, especially with Micah’s medical needs.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“That’s not good.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He paused. “It’s not good. Calvin wants so much to stay with his brother that he didn’t even try to pretend it didn’t scare him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy was quiet for a moment. Then, softly: “What if we take in Calvin?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked up, surprised.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I mean… just until a long-term placement’s found,” she continued. “We have the space. We’re stable. You already have a bond with him. He trusts you.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He stared at her. “You’re saying we… take him in?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“I’m saying maybe we’re exactly what he needs … even if it’s temporary.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax leaned back, exhaling hard. “It’s not that I don’t want to. But there’s a line. Between the badge and personal involvement.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You already crossed it the moment you stepped into that apartment and saw Micah on that couch,” she said. “And I’m glad you did.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He was quiet. Processing. His eyes moved to the hallway, down toward the spare room. It had been empty since the miscarriage.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy followed his gaze. “It’s not about replacing anyone. It’s about not turning away from someone who needs help now.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax’s voice was rough when it came. “I don’t want to give him something safe, only to have it ripped away again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We won’t make promises we can’t keep,” she said. “But we can give him what he hasn’t had in a long time — a home where no one leaves in the middle of the night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed, his emotions on the fence. And she could see it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“David, we are not taking him forever, just until Greer can place both of the boys together.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He looked at her. Studied her face. The steadiness in her eyes. The same strength that had steadied him through storms of his own.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, he nodded. “Okay. Let’s talk to Greer, and we need to talk to the captain.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Sandy reached across the table and took his hand. “We’ll take it one day at a time.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax looked down at their joined hands.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">For the first time in weeks, the weight in his chest loosened … just a little.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P31" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T8">Chapter 15</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Vellum_20_Chapter_20_Title" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="15_Somebodys_Watching_Me"/><text:span text:style-name="T8">15 - Somebody&apos;s Watching Me</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="15_Somebodys_Watching_Me"/><text:span text:style-name="T8"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="Its_Harvey_Johnson"/><text:span text:style-name="T9">The</text:span><text:bookmark-end text:name="Its_Harvey_Johnson"/><text:span text:style-name="T9"> CSU lab was dim, lit mostly by screens and the dull blue glow of a halogen task light. It smelled faintly of scorched coffee and static. Jax followed Lozano into the room, nodding at Frank Becker, who sat hunched at his usual workstation.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“You’re early,” Becker said without turning. “Good. You’ll want time for this.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano leaned on the desk. “Footage from the car wash?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Enhanced and stabilized.” Becker tapped a key. “Motion blur reduced, ambient light adjusted. This took all night.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stepped closer, watching the monitor. “Let’s see it.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The footage rolled silently. A shadow darted by the front of the car wash, coat flapping. Becker slowed the frames.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Watch here,” he said. “Frame 463.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The figure paused for a moment, reacting to something. A shift of movement. A car’s reflection. A noise from the street.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">The man turned his head slightly — just enough.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Becker froze the frame.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">There it was: a pale face, partially shadowed, but caught in the sliver of alley light. Short brown hair. Clean jawline. Eyes that didn’t blink, even in freeze-frame. For a fraction of a second, the man looked directly at the camera. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax stared. His stomach dropped.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano looked at him sideways. “You recognize him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax didn’t answer right away. The silence pulled long.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Jax?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax said nothing, studying the face. His mind swirled. The seconds burned.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano was starting to get frustrated. “Come on, Jax. Give me something!”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Frank ignored Lozano and handed Jax a print of frame 463. “Here. You’ll need this.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax ignored her plea. “Frank, please put all this together and send it to us. Can you print that picture out right now?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax grabbed it and left the room, Lozano in pursuit. “Where are we going, Jax?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">He didn’t answer, but hit the elevator to the fourth floor, and strode to the captain’s office, and knocked insistently.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Enter!” Holcomb answered.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax was agitated. “We have a problem, boss.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We?” Lozano asked. “He’s gone silent on me here.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Jax?” Holcomb pushed.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Look at this picture. This is the shooter of Evan Dixon and the chief.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax held the image out for the captain to take, and Lozano saw his face go pale.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“My God!” Holcomb shook his head in surprise and dropped the still onto his desk.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Yeah, it’s him.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb didn’t blink. “Where was this taken?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax sighed, “CSU enhanced footage from the car wash near the Dixon shooting. And here he is, clear as day. This was frame 463.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb shook his head. “How sure are you?”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax went to the whiteboard and quickly sketched out a simple map, and with a different colored pen, started drawing the route the figure took.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“He ran out of the building fifteen seconds after the shots were fired. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="normal111">* * *</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“We’ve confirmed the timeline. He ran out of the building seconds after the shot was fired. T</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb looked again at the photo, his hand tightening around the paper. “That’s not possible. He was supposed to be—”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Holcomb shook his head. “So, somewhere in the investigation, someone didn’t do their job.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">* * *</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stepped forward. “We’ve confirmed the timeline. He ran out of the building seconds after the shot was fired.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Lozano stood quietly by as the moments ticked away.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Finally, Holcomb looked up. “We need to get the task force back together again.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">Jax nodded. “And if Nicky is up to it, he needs to be there as well.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13" loext:marker-style-name="T2"><text:span text:style-name="T9">“Agreed. Let me start forming that task force again, and you brief Lozano and call Nicky.”</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"/></text:p>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
</office:document>